2025-12-04 06:55:14 [nitro@ryzen5] detect long lines in vscode
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] To detect long lines in Visual Studio Code, enable the ruler setting:
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 1. Open VSCode settings (Ctrl+, on Windows/Linux, Cmd+, on Mac).
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 2. Search for "editor.rulers".
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 3. Set it to an array of column numbers, e.g., `[80, 120]` to show vertical lines at those
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] positions.
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] This visually highlights lines exceeding your preferred length. For automated detection or linting,
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] consider extensions like ESLint or Prettier, which can flag long lines in code. If you need code
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] examples, please specify.
2025-12-04 06:55:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 06:55:40 [nitro@ryzen5] search for lines longer than 8000
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] In Visual Studio Code, use regex search to find lines longer than 8000 characters:
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 1. Open the search panel (Ctrl+Shift+F).
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 2. Enable regex mode (click the .* button).
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 3. Enter this regex: `^.{8001,}$`
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 4. Search in your workspace or specific files.
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] This matches any line with 8001+ characters. If you need a script for automated detection (e.g., in
2025-12-04 06:55:43 [grok-code-1] Node.js), request it.
