2025-12-04 10:33:37 [nitro@ryzen5] names for commodore serial bus
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] The Commodore Serial Bus is commonly known by these names:
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] - Commodore Serial Bus (CSB)
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] - IEC Bus (short for IEEE-488, though simplified)
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] - Serial Bus (in Commodore manuals and context)
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] It's used for peripherals like disk drives (e.g., 1541) on systems such as C64, VIC-20, PET. If you
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] need more details or code examples, let me know.
2025-12-04 10:33:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:34:12 [nitro@ryzen5] IEC after the 240' connector?
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] The IEC Bus (Commodore Serial Bus) uses a 6-pin DIN 45322 connector, often keyed at 240° for proper
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] orientation (notch position ensures correct plugging). "IEC" stands for International
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] Electrotechnical Commission, the standardizing body for the bus protocol.
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] No specific "after" the connector—it's the serial interface itself. If you mean something else
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] (e.g., pinout or wiring), provide more details.
2025-12-04 10:34:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:34:56 [nitro@ryzen5] IEC connector DIN 45322 spacing 240'
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] The IEC Bus connector is a 6-pin DIN 45322 (female socket, chassis-mount). The "240° spacing"
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] refers to the keying notch position—likely at 240° from a reference point on the connector body to
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] ensure correct orientation and prevent misinsertion (e.g., notch aligns with pin 1).
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] Standard pinout (from Commodore docs):
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] - 1: /SRQ
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] - 2: GND
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] - 3: /ATN
2025-12-04 10:35:14 [grok-code-1] - 4: /CLK
2025-12-04 10:35:15 [grok-code-1] - 5: /DATA
2025-12-04 10:35:15 [grok-code-1] - 6: /RESET
2025-12-04 10:35:15 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:35:15 [grok-code-1] If you need sourcing details or drawings, let me know.
2025-12-04 10:35:15 [grok-code-1] 
