2025-12-04 10:53:29 [nitro@ryzen5] can it spool paper, could you give me a simple design summary of it owkring
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] Yes, the Pico Plotter can spool/advance paper linearly using the Y drive motor and rubber feed
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] roller—paper feeds forward for plotting, but it's not designed for full rolling spooling (like a
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] receipt printer); add a take-up spool if needed.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] Simple Design Summary (How It Works):
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 1. Setup: RP2040 Pico controls motors/servos via GPIO. PCB mounts components in 52x35mm
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] footprint. Power via 5V USB/JST.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 2. X-Axis Movement: Coreless gearmotor drives GT2 belt/pulley for horizontal pen carriage
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] (precision ~0.5mm steps).
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 3. Y-Axis (Paper Feed): Gearmotor or stepper rotates knurled roller to advance paper
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] vertically.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 4. Pen Control: Micro servo lifts/lowers pen (e.g., gel pen) for drawing.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 5. Feedback: Endstop switches detect home position and paper presence.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 6. Operation: PIO generates motor pulses for smooth movement. Software parses simple
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] commands (e.g., lines via G-code) to plot on 20-30mm wide paper. Draws basic graphics emulating
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 1520 style.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] Total power ~500mA; runtime ~10-30 min on LiPo. For code or refinements, let me know.
2025-12-04 10:53:38 [grok-code-1] 
