2025-12-04 14:13:34 [grok-code-1] The Commodore 16 uses the TED (Text Editing Device) chip for video output, supporting composite
2025-12-04 14:13:34 [grok-code-1] video with 320x200 resolution in 128 colors (from a palette of 121). It connects via RF or
2025-12-04 14:13:34 [grok-code-1] composite to a TV or monitor, similar to the VIC-20 but with enhanced graphics capabilities. If you
2025-12-04 14:13:34 [grok-code-1] meant something else (like a demo video), clarify!
2025-12-04 14:13:34 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:14:00 [nitro@ryzen5] more palette! nice, with c64 palette, it is fixed 16?
2025-12-04 14:14:06 [grok-code-1] Yes, the C64's VIC-II chip uses a fixed 16-color palette (out of a possible 4096, but only 16
2025-12-04 14:14:06 [grok-code-1] selectable). In contrast, the C16's TED chip offers a more flexible 121-color palette with better
2025-12-04 14:14:06 [grok-code-1] color depth and no strict limit on simultaneous on-screen colors—allowing for richer graphics in
2025-12-04 14:14:06 [grok-code-1] some cases. Great catch on the palette comparison! If you'd like details on specific colors or
2025-12-04 14:14:06 [grok-code-1] examples, let me know.
