2025-12-04 14:14:27 [nitro@ryzen5] how do you select color on vic-II
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] In the C64's VIC-II chip, colors are selected by writing values (0-15) to specific memory registers
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] using BASIC POKE commands or assembly. Here are key ones:
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] - Border color: POKE 53280, color_index (e.g., POKE 53280, 0 for black)
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] - Background color: POKE 53281, color_index
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] - Text color: POKE 646, color_index (for the current character set)
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] Color indices 0-15 correspond to: 0=black, 1=white, 2=red, 3=cyan, 4=purple, 5=green, 6=blue,
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 7=yellow, 8=orange, 9=brown, 10=pink, 11=dark grey, 12=grey, 13=light green, 14=light blue,
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 15=light grey.
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] For sprites or bitmaps, additional registers control per-element colors. If you need code examples
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] or more details, ask!
2025-12-04 14:14:44 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:15:13 [nitro@ryzen5] so fixed 16 , there is no notion of more colors ?
2025-12-04 14:15:30 [grok-code-1] Yes, the VIC-II chip is limited to a fixed palette of exactly 16 colors—no way to add or redefine
2025-12-04 14:15:30 [grok-code-1] them in hardware. Programmers often use techniques like dithering, color cycling, or multi-color
2025-12-04 14:15:30 [grok-code-1] modes to simulate more shades or effects, but the core palette remains 16. If you're interested in
2025-12-04 14:15:30 [grok-code-1] those tricks or how TED differs, let me know!
2025-12-04 14:15:30 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:16:16 [nitro@ryzen5] on luma how many different voltages are produced by palete? given sync is 0v and 0.3 is black level?
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] In PAL composite video from the VIC-II, the 16-color palette produces 8 distinct luma (brightness)
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] voltages, ranging from black (0.3V above sync, where sync is 0V) to white (typically 1V above black
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] level, so ~1.3V). Not all 16 colors have unique luma—some share levels (e.g., multiple colors at
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] the same brightness). This is due to the chip's design limiting luma quantization to 8 steps. If
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] you need the specific voltages or NTSC details, let me know!
2025-12-04 14:16:30 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:17:14 [nitro@ryzen5] i will be counting chroma frequency only
2025-12-04 14:17:19 [grok-code-1] Got it—focusing on chroma. The VIC-II outputs chroma using a subcarrier frequency of approximately
2025-12-04 14:17:19 [grok-code-1] 3.58 MHz for NTSC or 4.43 MHz for PAL, modulating hue and saturation onto the signal. Each of the
2025-12-04 14:17:19 [grok-code-1] 16 colors has a unique chroma phase and amplitude. If you're analyzing or need specifics on
2025-12-04 14:17:19 [grok-code-1] frequencies/phases per color, clarify your setup!
2025-12-04 14:17:19 [grok-code-1] 
