2025-12-04 14:19:58 [nitro@ryzen5] with just tick counter and luma how many comparators do i need to uniquely id all 16 colors
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] To uniquely identify all 16 C64 colors using only a tick counter (for chroma phase, giving one of
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] 16 possible values) and luma (analog voltage), you need comparators solely for the luma
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] component—since chroma phase is digitally counted.
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] The palette has 8 distinct luma voltage levels. To resolve which of the 8 levels the analog luma
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] signal is in, you need 7 comparators (one for each threshold between the 7 gaps in the sorted luma
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] values: e.g., between 0.00 and 0.22, 0.22 and 0.25, etc., up to 0.78 and 1.00).
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] This gives you a 3-bit code for luma (8 possibilities) + 4-bit code from the tick counter (16
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] phases), totaling unique IDs for all 16 colors. If your setup differs or you need threshold values,
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] let me know!
2025-12-04 14:20:42 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:21:21 [nitro@ryzen5] can i drop 4?
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] Yes, you can drop to 4 comparators. Since the 11 non-grey colors each have unique chroma phases
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] (ticks ≠ 0), the tick counter alone identifies them. For the 5 grey colors (all with ticks = 0),
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] you only need to distinguish their 5 distinct luma levels (0.00, 0.32, 0.49, 0.66, 1.00), which
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] requires 4 comparators (one per threshold between the 4 gaps). This keeps unique ID for all 16
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] colors with minimal hardware. Great optimization! If you need the specific thresholds, ask.
2025-12-04 14:21:31 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:22:14 [nitro@ryzen5] i will determine sync floor using comparator also?
2025-12-04 14:22:25 [grok-code-1] Yes, using a comparator to detect the sync floor (the 0V sync tip) is a good idea—it provides a
2025-12-04 14:22:25 [grok-code-1] stable reference for measuring luma voltages relative to sync (e.g., black level at ~0.3V above
2025-12-04 14:22:25 [grok-code-1] sync). This ensures accurate threshold comparisons for the 4-7 comparators on luma. If you're
2025-12-04 14:22:25 [grok-code-1] building hardware, a peak detector or comparator on the sync signal works well. Need circuit ideas?
2025-12-04 14:22:25 [grok-code-1] 
