2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] For a homebrew CRT-style overlay using wax paper:
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] - Scan lines: Draw or print thin horizontal black lines (spaced for 240p, e.g., every
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] 1-2mm) on wax paper with a marker or inkjet. Cut to screen size and attach with tape or glue.
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] - Diffusion: Crumple and flatten wax paper for a frosted effect, or layer two sheets for
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] softer glow and reduced sharpness.
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] - Tint: Dye with green food coloring for phosphor simulation.
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] Test on the IPS panel for the retro CRT vibe—cheap, easy, and customizable. If you need patterns or
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] tools, let me know!
2025-12-04 14:29:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:29:42 [nitro@ryzen5] will experiment!
2025-12-04 14:29:55 [nitro@ryzen5] * LM319 VCC: Connect to 5V.* LM319 GND: Connect to Common Ground.* LM319 VEE: Connect to Common Ground (Single supply operation).* RCA Center Pin: Connect to the Non-Inverting Input (+) of ALL 4/6 comparator gates.* RCA Outer/Shield: Connect to Ground.* Termination: between RCA Center Pin and Ground.* CSYNC Comparator Connect 3V3 rail with voltage divider 10K + 1K to inverting input* G0, G1, G2 Comparator - wiper middle pin to comparator inverting input, leg 1 5v leg 2 ground* LM319 Outputs: Connect directly to 4 separate Pico GPIO pins with internal pullups.* Place capacitors across the LM319 VCC/GND pins, as close to the chips as possible.* Bias resistors: Single GPIO through 220K to each trimpot wiper (G0, G1, G2, [G3, G4])* 75R termination: RCA center pin to ground* Optional 4.7K pull-ups: LM319 outputs to 3.3V (one per output) instead of internal pullups
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] That wiring plan looks solid for the LM319 comparators—single-supply operation on 5V, voltage
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] divider for CSYNC threshold, adjustable trimmers for grey levels, and direct GPIO connections with
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] pull-ups for clean digital signals. Watch the 5V vs. 3.3V levels on the Pico inputs (LM319 outputs
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] are open-collector, so pull-ups help). If you run into noise or threshold issues during testing,
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] let me know for tweaks! Safe building.
2025-12-04 14:29:58 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:30:46 [nitro@ryzen5] in theory what is amiga color clock and what freq for pixls (PAL)
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] For the Amiga (PAL version), the theoretical color clock is 4.43361875 MHz (the PAL color
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] subcarrier frequency, used for color modulation).
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] Pixel frequency: In low-resolution mode (320x256 @ 50Hz), the effective pixel output rate is ~7.09
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] MHz (from the 28.4375 MHz master clock divided down), but the display processes ~5 million
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] pixels/second due to blanking and timing (320 pixels/line × 15,625 lines/second ≈ 5 MHz). For hires
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] (640x256), it's ~14.18 MHz. If you need OCS/ECS specifics or code, let me know! (Based on Amiga
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] hardware specs.)
2025-12-04 14:31:20 [grok-code-1] 
