2025-12-05 14:24:05 [nitro@ryzen5] apple2 pixel clock
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] The Apple II's pixel clock is 14.31818 MHz (master oscillator frequency). This divides down
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] to produce the video timing:
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] - Color burst reference: 3.579545 MHz (14.31818 ÷ 4)
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] - Dot clock: ~14.31818 ÷ 14 ≈ 1.023 MHz (actually 14.31818 ÷ 14 = 1.022727 MHz)
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] - Horizontal scan rate: 15.7 kHz
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] - Vertical refresh: 60 Hz
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] This timing gives the Apple II its characteristic 280×192 high-resolution graphics (40×24 text in
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] 7×8 cells). The 1 MHz dot clock means each pixel is approximately 1 µs wide.
2025-12-05 14:24:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:25 [nitro@ryzen5] so 7ns?
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] Yes, approximately 7 ns per pixel.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 1.022727 MHz = 1,022,727 cycles/second
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] Period = 1 / 1,022,727 ≈ 0.9778 µs per cycle.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] Since each pixel corresponds to one cycle of the dot clock:
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 0.9778 µs = 977.8 ns per pixel.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] Wait — that’s ~978 ns, not 7 ns. Let me check the math again.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] The master oscillator is 14.31818 MHz.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] The dot clock is derived by dividing by 14:
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 14.31818 MHz ÷ 14 = 1.022727 MHz.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] Period = 1 / 1.022727×10⁶ ≈ 0.9778×10⁻⁶ s = 977.8 ns.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] So each pixel lasts about 978 ns, not 7 ns.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 7 ns would correspond to a ~143 MHz pixel clock, which is far faster than the Apple II’s hardware.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] If you meant the master clock period:
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 14.31818 MHz → period ≈ 69.8 ns.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] But that’s not the pixel clock; that’s the system clock from which the pixel clock is divided down.
2025-12-05 14:24:36 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:24:55 [nitro@ryzen5] how many pio instructions in that time?
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] For the 6502 in the Apple II:
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - CPU clock = 1.023 MHz (same as dot clock, derived from 14.31818 MHz ÷ 14)
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - Cycle time ≈ 977.8 ns
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - Most 6502 instructions take 2–7 cycles
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] So in one pixel time (~978 ns = 1 cycle):
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - 0–1 PIO (Programmed I/O) instructions could complete, depending on the instruction.
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] If you meant per scanline:
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - Horizontal active time ≈ 40 µs (40 characters × 1 µs per pixel × 7 pixels wide)
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - That’s about 40 CPU cycles
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] - Could execute roughly 6–20 PIO instructions in one scanline.
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] The Apple II’s video generation is cycle‑stealing — the CPU is halted during certain video
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] memory accesses, so timing is tight for cycle‑exact graphics effects.
2025-12-05 14:25:05 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:19 [nitro@ryzen5] rp2040 pio instructions in 7ns
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] The RP2040 PIO state machines run at the system clock (default up to 133 MHz, often 125
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] MHz).
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] At 125 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Cycle time = 8 ns
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Each PIO instruction takes 1 clock cycle (8 ns) for most instructions
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] In 7 ns:
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - You could execute 0–1 PIO instructions (7 ns < 8 ns cycle time)
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] At 133 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Cycle time ≈ 7.52 ns
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Still 0–1 instructions in 7 ns
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] At overclocked 250 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Cycle time = 4 ns
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] - Could execute 1 PIO instruction in 7 ns (with 3 ns leftover)
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] So practically: 1 PIO instruction per 7 ns is only possible with RP2040 overclocked to ≥ ~143
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] MHz (7 ns period). At typical 125 MHz, you need ~8 ns per instruction.
2025-12-05 14:25:29 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:26:56 [nitro@ryzen5] anyway to sync rp with a typical 14mhz video clock?
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can sync the RP2040 with a 14 MHz video clock using several approaches:
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 1. PIO Clock Input
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Use the PIO clock pin (`clk_pin`) to feed the 14 MHz video clock directly to a state machine
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - State machine runs at exactly the video clock rate
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Requires clean 50% duty cycle clock signal
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 2. Phase-Locked Loop (PLL)
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Use RP2040's PLL to generate a system clock that's a multiple of 14 MHz
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Example: 14 MHz × 9 = 126 MHz (close to 125 MHz default)
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Provides integer-ratio synchronization
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 3. Pixel Clock Recovery
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Use PIO to measure video sync pulses (HSYNC/VSYNC)
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Adjust PIO timing loops to match derived pixel clock
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Works with non-clock video sources (composite, etc.)
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 4. External Clock Generator
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Use external PLL/VCO to generate RP2040's clock from video source
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] - Most precise method for genlock applications
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] Example PIO Configuration:
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] from machine import Pin
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] @rp2.asm_pio()
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] def video_sync():
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]     wrap_target()
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]     pull(block)          # Wait for pixel data
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]     out(pins, 8)         # Output to DAC/VGA pins
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]     nop() [1]            # Timing adjustment
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]     wrap()
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] # Configure PIO to use external clock
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] sm = rp2.StateMachine(0, video_sync, freq=14_000_000,
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]                       out_base=Pin(0),
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat]                       set_base=Pin(8))
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] The RP2040's flexible clocking makes it well-suited for video timing synchronization.
2025-12-05 14:27:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:39 [nitro@ryzen5] yes 14 MHz x 9 = 126 MHz
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] Exactly. 14 MHz × 9 = 126 MHz gives you:
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - Integer ratio (9:1) between system clock and video clock
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - Each video pixel clock cycle = 9 system clock cycles
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - Allows cycle‑exact timing in PIO state machines
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] Practical Implementation:
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] from machine import Pin, freq
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] # Set system clock to 126 MHz (14 MHz × 9)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] freq(126_000_000)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] @rp2.asm_pio(out_init=rp2.PIO.OUT_LOW * 8,
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]              out_shiftdir=rp2.PIO.SHIFT_RIGHT,
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]              autopull=True,
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]              pull_thresh=32)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] def vga_pixel():
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]     wrap_target()
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]     out(pins, 8) [7]    # Output 8 bits, wait 7 cycles (total 8)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat]     wrap()
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] # 126 MHz / 8 = 15.75 MHz pixel clock
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] # Each pixel = 8 system cycles = 63.5 ns
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] # 14.31818 MHz (Apple II) would need different divider
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] For Apple II's 14.31818 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - 14.31818 × 9 = 128.86362 MHz (close to achievable)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - Or 14.31818 × 7 = 100.22726 MHz (simpler ratio)
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] - Use fractional PLL configuration in RP2040's clocks
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] The integer multiple ensures perfect synchronization between CPU/PIO timing and video generation.
2025-12-05 14:27:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:28:43 [nitro@ryzen5] i want to sync rp to the clock so it is in sync with edge of blanking periods
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] To sync the RP2040 to video blanking edges, you need to:
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 1. Clock Synchronization
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] Feed the video clock (14.31818 MHz) into the RP2040's clock input:
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] from machine import Pin, freq
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] # Use external clock on GPIO20 (CLK_GPIIN)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] rp2.bootsel_button()  # Ensure we can reconfigure clocks
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] # Configure to use external clock input
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] import machine
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] machine.freq(0)  # Let external clock drive system
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 2. HSYNC/VSYNC Detection with PIO
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] @rp2.asm_pio()
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] def sync_detector():
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Wait for HSYNC falling edge (start of active video)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     wait(0, pin, 0)  # Wait for pin low
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     wait(1, pin, 0)  # Wait for pin high again
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Reset pixel counter at exact HSYNC edge
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(x, 279)      # 280 pixels per line - 1
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     label("pixel_loop")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     jmp(x_dec, "pixel_loop")  # Count pixels
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Trigger blanking period action
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     irq(0)           # Signal CPU for end of line
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     wrap()
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 3. Precise Blanking Edge Alignment
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] @rp2.asm_pio(set_init=rp2.PIO.OUT_LOW)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] def blanking_generator():
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Align to external clock domain
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     wrap_target()
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Generate front porch (16 pixels)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(y, 15)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     label("front_porch")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     nop() [7]        # 8 cycles per pixel
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     jmp(y_dec, "front_porch")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Generate sync pulse (24 pixels)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(pins, 0)     # Assert sync
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(y, 23)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     label("sync_pulse")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     nop() [7]
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     jmp(y_dec, "sync_pulse")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Generate back porch (40 pixels)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(pins, 1)     # End sync
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(y, 39)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     label("back_porch")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     nop() [7]
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     jmp(y_dec, "back_porch")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     # Active video (280 pixels)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     set(y, 279)
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     label("active_video")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     out(pins, 8)     # Output pixel data
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     jmp(y_dec, "active_video")
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat]     wrap()
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 4. Phase Alignment Circuit
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] For perfect edge alignment, you may need:
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] - External PLL (74HC4046) to clean up video clock
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] - Delay line for sub-nanosecond adjustment
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] - D-type flip-flop to resync on HSYNC edge
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 5. RP2040 PLL Configuration
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] # Manually configure PLL for exact video multiple
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] import machine
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] pll = machine.PLL()
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] pll.configure(14_318_180 * 9)  # 128.86362 MHz
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] The key is using the video clock as the RP2040's clock source, then using PIO state machines that
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] are inherently synchronized to that clock domain. This ensures all timing edges align perfectly
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] with the video signal.
2025-12-05 14:29:11 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:39 [nitro@ryzen5] GPIO20 (CLK_GPIIN) is that fixed?
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] Yes, GPIO20 is fixed as the CLK_GPIIN (General Purpose Input Clock) on RP2040.
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] Clock Input Pins (Fixed Assignment):
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - GPIO20: `CLK_GPIIN` - General purpose clock input
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - GPIO21: `CLK_GPOUT` - General purpose clock output
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - GPIO24: `XIN` - Crystal oscillator input
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - GPIO25: `XOUT` - Crystal oscillator output
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] Configuration:
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] from machine import Pin
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] # Configure GPIO20 as clock input
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] import machine
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] machine.freq(0)  # Let external clock drive system
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] # Or configure PLL to use external reference
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] import rp2
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] rp2.PLL.configure(ref_freq=14_318_180,  # 14.31818 MHz input
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                   vco_freq=14_318_180 * 9,  # Multiply
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                   post_div1=6,
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                   post_div2=2)
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] Hardware Connection:
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] Video Clock (14.31818 MHz) ────┐
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                                │
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             ┌──▼──┐
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             │ 1kΩ │
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             └──┬──┘
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                                │
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             GPIO20 (CLK_GPIIN)
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                                │
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             ┌──▼──┐
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             │ 33pF│
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                             └──┬──┘
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                                │
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat]                               GND
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] The external clock must be:
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - 3.3V level (use level shifter if needed)
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - 50% duty cycle recommended
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] - Stable frequency (±100 ppm for video)
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] GPIO20 is the only pin that can directly drive the RP2040's clocking system without PIO
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] intervention.
2025-12-05 14:29:57 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] This is a well-documented project for capturing C64 video using RP2040. The design is solid with
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] clear GPIO allocation and comparator circuitry.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] Key Observations:
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 1. Video Capture Approach: Using LM319 comparators for 2-bit luma + sync is practical for
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] C64's analog video.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 2. GPIO Usage: Good allocation considering the LCD display constraints. You have 3 GPIOs
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] available (GP4, GP5, GP6, GP7) for future expansion.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 3. PIO Requirements: You'll need PIO state machines to:
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]    - Sample 4-6 comparator outputs synchronously
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]    - Detect sync pulses for frame/line timing
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]    - Buffer pixel data for 320×200 @ 50Hz
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] Potential Considerations:
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] - Sampling Rate: C64 pixel clock is ~8 MHz, requiring ~125 ns sampling. RP2040 at 125 MHz
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] (8 ns cycles) can handle this with PIO.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] - Memory: 320×200×2 bits = 16,000 bytes per frame, fits easily in RP2040 RAM.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] - Sync Detection: The fixed threshold for CSYNC should work well with C64's 0.3V sync tip.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] PIO Code Outline:
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] @rp2.asm_pio(in_shiftdir=rp2.PIO.SHIFT_RIGHT,
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]              autopush=True, push_thresh=32)
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] def capture_luma():
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]     wrap_target()
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]     in_(pins, 4)        # Read 4 comparator bits
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]     nop() [3]           # Timing adjustment for ~125 ns
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat]     wrap()
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] The design looks feasible and well-thought-out for C64 video capture.
2025-12-05 14:31:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:33:47 [nitro@ryzen5] is there simple socket where external video clock can be used on switch
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Yes, you can use a DIP switch or jumper header to select between internal and
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] external video clock:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Simple DIP Switch Configuration:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]         +5V
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]          │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     ┌────┴────┐
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     │  DIP-4  │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     │ 1  2  3 │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     └─┬─┬─┬─┬─┘
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]       │ │ │ │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]       │ │ │ └───┐
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]       │ │ │     │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]      ┌┴─┴─┴─┐   │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]      │74HC125│   │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]      │Buffer │   │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]      └┬─┬─┬──┘   │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]       │ │ │      │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Internal│ │External
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]  Clock  │ │ Clock
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] (14MHz) │ │(14.31818MHz)
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]         │ │      │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        GPIO20   GPIO20
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        (when    (when
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]         ext)     int)
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Jumper Header Alternative:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        ┌─────┬─────┐
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        │ INT │ EXT │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] GPIO20 ├─────┼─────┤ Video Clock In
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        │  ○  │  ○  │
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]        └─────┴─────┘
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Circuit Details:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] - 74HC125 tri-state buffer (quad)
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] - DIP switch selects which clock source drives GPIO20
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] - Internal clock from RP2040's own oscillator
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] - External clock from video source via BNC/RCA
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] Code to Detect:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] from machine import Pin
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] clock_select = Pin(22, Pin.IN, Pin.PULL_UP)  # DIP switch pin
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] if clock_select.value():  # External clock selected
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     # Configure for external clock input
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     machine.freq(0)  # Let external clock drive
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] else:
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     # Use internal PLL
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat]     machine.freq(126_000_000)  # 14MHz × 9
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] This gives you a simple, hardware-based clock source selection.
2025-12-05 14:34:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:00 [nitro@ryzen5] generate stable 8mhz clock from composite video input or just luma
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] You can generate a stable 8 MHz clock from composite video or just luma using:
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 1. PLL Recovery Circuit (Most Stable)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] Composite/Luma ──┬─── 0.1µF ────┐
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                  │              │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                 75Ω             │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                  │              │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                 GND          74HC4046
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                                  │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                              8 MHz ────► GPIO20
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] - 74HC4046 PLL locks to horizontal sync (15.7 kHz × 455 = 7.14 MHz)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] - Adjust loop filter for 8 MHz output
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 2. Crystal-Locked Multiplier
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] Luma ──► LM1881 ──► HSYNC (15.7 kHz)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                      │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                  74HC4040 ──► ÷455 ──► 7.14 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                      │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                  14.31818 MHz Crystal
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                      │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                  74HC74 ×2 ──► 8 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 3. RP2040 PLL Software Recovery
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] import machine
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] import time
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] # Measure HSYNC period
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] hsync = Pin(14, Pin.IN)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] last_time = time.ticks_us()
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] while True:
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     while hsync.value(): pass  # Wait for low
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     while not hsync.value(): pass  # Wait for high
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     period = time.ticks_diff(time.ticks_us(), last_time)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     last_time = time.ticks_us()
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     # Adjust PLL to maintain 8 MHz = period × 455
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     target_freq = 1_000_000 / period * 455
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]     machine.freq(int(target_freq))
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 4. Direct Color Burst Extraction (Composite Only)
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] Composite ──► Bandpass 3.58 MHz ──► Comparator ──►
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                                               │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                                            ×2 ──► 7.16 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                                            │
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat]                                         ×9/8 ──► 8.055 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] - Color burst is stable 3.579545 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] - Multiply to get close to 8 MHz
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] Simplest Working Solution:
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] Use LM1881 to extract sync, then 74HC4046 to generate 8 MHz locked to HSYNC. This
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] gives stable clock even from noisy luma signals.
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] For just luma, the color burst isn't available, so HSYNC-based recovery is your only option.
2025-12-05 14:35:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:28 [nitro@ryzen5] 4046 Phase Lock Loop CMOS ICCAT.NO: ZC4046
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] The ZC4046 (CMOS 4046 Phase-Locked Loop) is perfect for this application. Here's the
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] configuration:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Circuit for 8 MHz from Composite/Luma:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Composite/Luma ────┬─── 0.1µF ──── IN (Pin 14)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                    │
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                   75Ω
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                    │
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                   GND
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] ZC4046 Connections:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 14: Signal Input (from video)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 3: Comparator Input (connect to Pin 4 for VCO feedback)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 4: VCO Output (8 MHz)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 9: VCO Control Voltage (to loop filter)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 6, 7: Timing Caps/Resistors for 8 MHz center
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 16: VDD (+5V)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 8: VSS (GND)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Loop Filter:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 9 ── 10kΩ ──┬── 0.1µF ── GND
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                  │
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                 1MΩ
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                  │
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat]                 GND
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] VCO Timing:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 6 ── 100pF ── GND
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 7 ── 10kΩ ─── VDD
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 11 ── 10kΩ ── VDD (optional for offset)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Output:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Pin 4 (8 MHz) ── 100Ω ── GPIO20 (RP2040 CLK_GPIIN)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Component Values for 8 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - R1 (Pin 11): 10kΩ (offset resistor, optional)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - R2 (Pin 7): 10kΩ (sets frequency range)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - C1 (Pin 6): 100pF (timing capacitor)
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - VCO Center: f ≈ 1/(R2 × C1) ≈ 1/(10k × 100p) = 1 MHz
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - Actual 8 MHz needs lower R2 or C1 values
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] For Exact 8 MHz:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Try:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - R2: 1.2kΩ
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - C1: 100pF
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] - f_center ≈ 1/(1.2k × 100p) ≈ 8.33 MHz
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] Locking to Video:
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] The ZC4046 will lock its VCO to the horizontal sync frequency (15.7 kHz) and multiply it by ~455 to
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] get 7.14 MHz, then adjust slightly to 8 MHz based on your R2/C1 values.
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] This gives you a clean, stable 8 MHz clock synchronized to the video source.
2025-12-05 14:36:51 [deepseek-chat] 
