2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] If the file is written but the image is still corrupted, the next most likely culprit is the
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] LZW minimum code-size byte that you emit inside the image descriptor:
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] this.#bytes.push(...this.#gce(delay), 0x2C,
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905]   ...u16le(this.#px), ...u16le(this.#py),
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905]   ...u16le(this.#w), ...u16le(this.#h),
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905]   0x00, 0x08);   // ← 0x08 is wrong here
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] That final `0x08` is being interpreted as the start of the LZW data block, but it is
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] not the sub-block count—it's the LZW minimum code size.
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Because you pack two 4-bit pixels per byte, the symbols you feed to LZW are 0-255, so the correct
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] minimum code size is 8, not 5.
2025-12-06 10:52:45 [kimi-k2-0905] (Your compressor starts with code-size 5, but that is an internal detail; the GIF header byte must
