2025-12-08 10:34:24 [nitro@ryzen5] only 64738 had any effect???
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] The other addresses are inside the KERNAL ROM; they only work if:
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. The KERNAL ROM is currently mapped in (bit 2 of $01 = 1), and
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. You jump to them with JMP (or `SYS`) while the CPU is still in KERNAL context
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] (i.e. no cartridge has stolen the reset vector, no fast-load wedge has overwritten the vectors,
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] etc.).
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] If you’re in BASIC and type `SYS 65517` and nothing happens, the most common reason is that
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] the warm-reset vector at $FFFC has been overwritten by a DOS wedge, fast-loader, or cartridge.
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Try the raw jump from machine-language instead:
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] POKE 788,91: POKE 789,255: SYS 64738
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] That patches the “default” warm-reset entry point ($FF5B) back into the vector table and then
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] resets—every un-wedged C64 will obey it.
2025-12-08 10:34:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
