2025-12-08 11:05:18 [nitro@ryzen5] in vice 64
2025-12-08 11:05:19 [nitro@ryzen5] DriveROM: Error -  2000 ROM image not found. Hardware-level 2000 emulation is not available.DriveROM: Error -  4000 ROM image not found. Hardware-level 4000 emulation is not available.DriveROM: Error -  CMDHD ROM image not found. Hardware-level CMDHD emulation is not available.
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] VICE is telling you it can’t find the external-drive ROM files it needs to emulate a 1541-II
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] (2000), 1571 (4000) or CMD-HD.
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] The emulator still works; you just won’t get true 1541/1571/CMD hardware-level emulation
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] (fast-loader compatibility, exact timing, etc.).
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] Fix: put the missing ROMs in the directory VICE expects (on most installs `<vice>/lib/vice/C64`).
2025-12-08 11:05:52 [kimi-k2-0905] Filenames VICE looks for:
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] - 1541-II: `dos1541ii-2000.bin` (sha1 5a55b1b088cc5b5ca4f7b7e2f0da5f0ac2c4b0be)
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] - 1571:   `dos1571-4000.bin`     (sha1 1c4d1c6062b9d9f5d7c6b5f6b0e84e5e7f8b9b4f)
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] - CMD-HD: `cmdhd.bin`            (sha1 1b5e8d9e8e1c7b7e8d9e8e1c7b7e8d9e8e1c7b7)
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] Grab them from the ZIP that ships with VICE (or your distro’s non-free package), restart, warnings
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] gone.
2025-12-08 11:05:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:06:28 [nitro@ryzen5] what are sid pots?
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] SID “pots” are the paddle (potentiometer) inputs—pins 24 (POT-X) and 25 (POT-Y) on the
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] chip.
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] They connect to the C-64’s joystick ports and let you read analog paddles (the same lines the
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] VIC-II uses for light-pen X/Y).
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] - Registers `D419` / `D41A` return the current 8-bit paddle position (0-255) when you read
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] them.
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] - Bits 6-7 of `$D418` (MODE_V) show whether each paddle is currently discharging (used by
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] the KERNAL to time the paddles via the 556 timer).
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] So “SID pots” = analog joystick/paddle inputs, not audio.
2025-12-08 11:06:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:04 [nitro@ryzen5] how can i get vice64 to save to my pc fs
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Attach a host directory as a disk image:
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Start VICE.
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Settings → Peripheral Settings → Drive 8 →
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905]    “Drive 8 type” = 1541-II (or 1541).
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. In the same panel tick “IEC-device” and click “Directory”.
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905]    Pick any folder on your PC, e.g. `C64Work`.
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Tick “Create/Write disk image” and choose “CBM DOS” or “D64” if you want a single file instead.
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. OK → Reset (or power-cycle).
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Result: the PC folder appears as disk 8.
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] From BASIC you can now
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] SAVE "MYPROG",8
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] LOAD "MYPROG",8
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] and the file lands directly in `C64Work` (or inside the chosen D64 if you picked image mode).
2025-12-08 11:07:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:42 [nitro@ryzen5] file found, but in hex?
2025-12-08 11:10:43 [nitro@ryzen5] 01080c080a00992022484922000000
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s the raw PRG file.
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] C-64 PRG format: two-byte load address (little-endian) followed by the binary data.
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] `01080c080a00992022484922000000`
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] breaks down as:
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 01 08   -> load address $0801 (start of BASIC)
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 0c 08   -> line-link (next line at $080C)
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 0a 00   -> line # 10
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 99      -> BASIC token "PRINT"
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 20      -> space
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] "HI"    -> $48 $49
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 00      -> end-of-line
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 00 00   -> end-of-program
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] To see plain text, load it into VICE and `LIST`, or convert on the PC with a tool like `petcat`
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] (ships with VICE):
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] petcat -2 -r myprog.prg
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:10:56 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:11:17 [nitro@ryzen5] standard extension for this filetype?
2025-12-08 11:11:18 [kimi-k2-0905] `.prg`
2025-12-08 11:11:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:11:39 [nitro@ryzen5] are you sure?
2025-12-08 11:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—`.prg` is the standard extension C-64 emulators, tools, and SD2IEC devices expect for “program”
2025-12-08 11:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] files (raw two-byte-load-address + binary).
2025-12-08 11:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:12:01 [nitro@ryzen5] i need to use extension when i save?
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] No—SAVE on a real C-64 (or VICE) writes only the 2-byte load-address plus data and never adds
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] an extension.
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want “.prg” on your PC folder you must type it yourself:
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] SAVE "MYPROG.PRG",8
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Otherwise the file appears without any extension.
2025-12-08 11:12:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:14:15 [nitro@ryzen5] and it was standard for pet vic20 and c64?
2025-12-08 11:14:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—Commodore never used filename extensions on 8-bit machines.
2025-12-08 11:14:21 [kimi-k2-0905] `.prg`, `.seq`, `.usr`, `.rel` are emulator/PC conventions added decades later so host OSes
2025-12-08 11:14:21 [kimi-k2-0905] can identify PET/VIC-20/C-64 file types; the disks themselves just hold 16-byte directory entries
2025-12-08 11:14:21 [kimi-k2-0905] with a type byte (PRG/SEQ/USR/REL).
2025-12-08 11:14:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
