2025-12-08 11:17:58 [nitro@ryzen5] why spc and tab have (
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Because the tokenizer stores them as two-byte sequences:
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - `$A6` (`166`) → `"SPC("`
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - `$A3` (`163`) → `"TAB("`
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] The opening parenthesis is part of the single token so BASIC can parse `SPC(10)` or `TAB(20)`
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] without a separate `"("` token.
2025-12-08 11:18:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:19:31 [nitro@ryzen5] GO command, maybe goto?
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] `GO` is a real one-byte token (`$CB`, 203).
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] BASIC accepts both spellings:
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] GO TO 100      ← two keywords: GO + TO
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] GOTO 100       ← one keyword: GOTO
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] The tokenizer keeps them distinct; `GO` exists only so the two-word form can be reconstructed when
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] LISTing.
2025-12-08 11:19:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:20:00 [nitro@ryzen5] is go 100 valid?
2025-12-08 11:20:04 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-12-08 11:20:04 [kimi-k2-0905] `GO` must be followed by `TO` (or `SUB`) to form a complete statement; `GO 100` produces `?SYNTAX
2025-12-08 11:20:04 [kimi-k2-0905] ERROR`.
2025-12-08 11:20:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:20:30 [nitro@ryzen5] but gosub is single token???
2025-12-08 11:20:35 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—`GOSUB` is a single token (`$8D`, 141).
2025-12-08 11:20:35 [kimi-k2-0905] The two-word form is `GO SUB` (token 203 followed by token 164), which the tokenizer also accepts
2025-12-08 11:20:35 [kimi-k2-0905] and LIST will show as `GO SUB`.
2025-12-08 11:20:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:21:11 [nitro@ryzen5] token 164 is to not sub
2025-12-08 11:21:18 [kimi-k2-0905] You’re right—token 164 is `TO`, not `SUB`.
2025-12-08 11:21:18 [kimi-k2-0905] There is no separate `SUB` token; `GO SUB` is simply not implemented in C-64 BASIC.
2025-12-08 11:21:18 [kimi-k2-0905] Only these two spellings exist:
2025-12-08 11:21:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:21:19 [kimi-k2-0905] - `GOTO`  (single token 137)
2025-12-08 11:21:19 [kimi-k2-0905] - `GOSUB` (single token 141)
2025-12-08 11:21:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:21:19 [kimi-k2-0905] So `GO SUB 100` produces `?SYNTAX ERROR`; the two-word form works only for `GO TO`.
2025-12-08 11:21:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 11:22:24 [nitro@ryzen5] any late entries?
2025-12-08 11:22:25 [nitro@ryzen5] c64_tokens={	"END":128,"FOR":129,"NEXT":130,"DATA":131,"INPUT#":132,"INPUT":133,"DIM":134,"READ":135,"LET":136,	"GOTO":137,"RUN":138,	"IF":139,"RESTORE":140,	"GOSUB":141,"RETURN":142,"REM":143,"STOP":144,"ON":145,"WAIT":146,"LOAD":147,	"SAVE":148,"VERIFY":149,"DEF":150,"POKE":151,"PRINT#":152,"PRINT":153,"CONT":154,"LIST":155,	"CLR":156,"CMD":157,"SYS":158,"OPEN":159,"CLOSE":160,"GET":161,"NEW":162,"TAB(":163,	"TO":164,"FN":165,"SPC(":166,"THEN":167,"NOT":168,"STEP":169,"+":170,"-":171,"*":172,	"/":173,"":174,"AND":175,"OR":176,">":177,"=":178,"<":179,"SGN":180,"INT":181,	"ABS":182,"USR":183,"FRE":184,"POS":185,"SQR":186,"RND":187,"LOG":188,"EXP":189,	"COS":190,"SIN":191,"TAN":192,"ATN":193,"PEEK":194,"LEN":195,"STR$":196,"VAL":197,	"ASC":198,"CHR$":199,"LEFT$":200,"RIGHT$":201,"MID$":202,	"GO":203};
