2025-12-08 17:22:18 [nitro@ryzen5] so are there 3 16 bit words at address 0x?
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] The three 16-bit values you see are not stored at address 0x0000; they are the BASIC
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] loader stub that starts at $0801 (2049 decimal).
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] - 0801  .word  next-line-link, 2016   ; 2016 = 07E0
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] - $0805  .word  0                     ; end-of-BASIC marker
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] - $0807  …your machine-code program…
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] The C-64 BASIC interpreter sees those first two words as a tiny BASIC line that says “SYS 2064”
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] (because 2064 = $0810, the address of your machine code).
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] Zero-page (0000–00FF) is left untouched by this header.
2025-12-08 17:22:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
