2025-12-08 17:28:56 [nitro@ryzen5] why should labels have own lines? is that a rule?
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] The assembler needs to see the label before it starts parsing the mnemonic or directive on
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] that line.
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] If you write
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] stub  .word 0
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] the parser meets the token “stub”, decides it’s a label, and then expects either
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] end-of-line or a new statement.
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] When you instead glue it to the previous statement like
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] .word stub,2064stub .word 0
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] the lexer sees one long token “2064stub” and throws an “unknown symbol” error.
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Putting each label on its own line is therefore required by every 6502/6510 assembler I
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] know (64tass, ACME, TMPx, ca65, etc.).
2025-12-08 17:29:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
