2025-12-09 05:44:57 [nitro@ryzen5] PRG header step
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] Here’s a small PRG header wrapper and how to apply it when writing assembled code.
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] Code (add to PETSYNC):
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \tfunction wrapPRG(data: Uint8Array, loadAddress: number = 0x0801): Uint8Array {
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t\tconst header = new Uint8Array([loadAddress & 0xFF, (loadAddress >> 8) & 0xFF]);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t\tconst out = new Uint8Array(header.length + data.length);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t\tout.set(header, 0);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t\tout.set(data, header.length);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t\treturn out;
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \t}
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] Usage (in the assembly path):
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \tconst obj:Uint8Array=assemble6510(asm);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \tconst asmSync="."+entry.name.slice(0,-4);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \tconst asmPath=join(floppy,asmSync);
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] \tawait Deno.writeFile(asmPath, wrapPRG(obj, 0x0801));
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] Notes:
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] - This wraps assembled code with the standard PRG header (load address 0x0801 by default).
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] - For BASIC/PRG outputs already produced by encodePRG, you can keep using the existing bytes (they
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] already include the header).
2025-12-09 05:45:27 [gpt-5-nano] 
