2025-12-09 06:33:17 [nitro@ryzen5] what do standard c64 assemblers do when producing prg files?
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] Short answer:
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - Standard C64 assemblers usually output a PRG as a binary blob and prefix it with a 2-byte load
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] address (low byte, high byte). The file can then be loaded directly by the CBM DOS loader.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] What they typically do
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - Two formats you’ll see:
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano]   - PRG with header: [loadlo, loadhi, code bytes…]
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano]   - Raw BIN: just the machine code bytes (no address), intended to be wrapped later by a
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] linker/script.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - Load address usually comes from the origin/orig directive in the source (e.g., ORG 0801 for BASIC
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] start; ORG 0600, $1000, etc. for machine code).
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - For BASIC programs, a PRG can encode the tokenized BASIC data (lines with 2-byte pointers, line
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] numbers, tokens) starting at 0x0801. For pure machine code, the PRG header simply points to the
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] start address of the code.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - Many toolchains (cc65/ld65, DASM, ACME, DASM-like tools) offer:
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano]   - An option to emit PRG directly (header preprended).
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano]   - Or to emit raw BIN and let you wrap with a helper (like wrapPRG) in a build step.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - If you’re assembling into multiple segments, you typically produce one PRG per segment or supply
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] a small loader that copies each segment to its target address.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] Practical tip
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] - If you want maximum compatibility, generate PRG files by wrapping the assembled bytes with a
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] 2-byte load address (start address) in little-endian, e.g. 01 08 for 0x0801.
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] Would you like a quick patch snippet to auto-wrap assembled output into PRG using your existing
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] wrapPRG, plus a naming convention for the output files?
2025-12-09 06:33:49 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:39:29 [nitro@ryzen5] what is decimal $801
2025-12-09 06:39:45 [gpt-5-nano] 2049.
2025-12-09 06:39:45 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:39:45 [gpt-5-nano] Reason: $801 is hex 0x801, which equals 8256 + 016 + 1 = 2048 + 1 = 2049 decimal. Note that
2025-12-09 06:39:45 [gpt-5-nano] 0x0801 is the same value (2049); the PRG header stores it as little-endian bytes 01 08.
2025-12-09 06:39:45 [gpt-5-nano] 
