2025-12-09 06:44:16 [nitro@ryzen5] does ffd2 corrupt x?
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] Short answer: yes, JSR $FFD2 (the CHROUT kernal call) can clobber X. It’s not guaranteed to
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] preserve X across the call.
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] How to preserve X around the call (minimal pattern)
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] - Save X to the accumulator and back around the JSR:
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano]   - TXA
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano]   - PHA
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano]   - JSR $FFD2
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano]   - PLA
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano]   - TAX
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] Reason: you push the value of X onto the stack, let CHROUT run, then restore X from A back into X.
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] If you need to call multiple times or from tight loops, you can wrap this pattern in a tiny macro
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] or function in your assembler.
2025-12-09 06:44:42 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:46:33 [nitro@ryzen5] ; tiny4.asm; sys 2049	* = $0801	start	ldx	#0loop	txa      pha      lda	message,x	beq	done	jsr	$ffd2      pla      tax	inx	bne	loopdone	rtsmessage .byte "HELLO",13,0
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] Looks good. The updated snippet correctly preserves X around the CHROUT (KERNAL print) call by:
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Saving X: TXA
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Pushing it: PHA
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Printing: LDA message,X; JSR $FFD2
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Restoring X: PLA; TAX
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Then incrementing X and looping
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] Notes:
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - Ensure the ORG is 0x0801 and you wrap the PRG with a 2-byte load address header if you’re loading
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] from BASIC SYS 2049.
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] - The string ends with 13,0 (CR and NUL). If you want a newline, add 10 (LF) as well.
2025-12-09 06:47:05 [gpt-5-nano] 
