2025-12-09 07:18:16 [nitro@ryzen5] what commands work with prg files on c64
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] Here are the common ways to use PRG files on a Commodore 64 (real hardware or emulator):
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] - BASIC workflow (for ML or BASIC+ML PRGs)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - LOAD"FILENAME",8,1
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - SYS <start-address>  (start-address is the value in the PRG header; e.g., 0xC000 -> 49152)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - If the PRG is a pure BASIC program, you’d use RUN instead of SYS.
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] - PRG that’s purely binary (ML)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - After LOAD"FILENAME",8,1, run it with SYS <start-address> as above.
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] - Saving a memory region as a PRG
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - BSAVE"FILENAME",start,end
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - The PRG will load back to the same address when BLOAD’ed later.
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] - Loading a raw binary without a header (less common)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - BLOAD"FILENAME",<start-address>  (loads binary data to the given address)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - Then SYS <start-address> to execute
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] - Using an emulator (e.g., VICE)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - Autostart: File -> Autostart PRG (or drag-and-drop the PRG)
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - Manual load/run: use the BASIC LOAD and SYS steps above
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano]   - Some emulators also support a quick “Run PRG” option when you select the file
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] 
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] If you share your exact setup (model, emulator, or whether the PRG is pure ML or BASIC), I can give
2025-12-09 07:18:51 [gpt-5-nano] precise, tool-specific commands.
