2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] A few things worth noting so the list lines up with what the 64 actually tokenises:
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - CBM BASIC 2.0 has no token for AUTO, BSAVE, BLOAD, CLS, READ#, WRITE#, ELSE, or GO by itself –
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] those either don’t exist or are handled differently.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - Functions that return a string exist only in their -form (CHR, STR, LEFT, RIGHT, MID); the bare
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] “CHR”, “STR”, “LEFT”, “RIGHT” you show aren’t recognised.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - TAB and SPC are tokenised as TAB( and SPC( (the opening parenthesis is part of the token).
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - Operators such as +, –, *, /, ^, =, >, < are single-byte tokens, but they’re not keywords you
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] type as commands; they’re absorbed during expression parsing.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - VERIFY is indeed a token, but it’s missing from your opener.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] - The last two items in your set (“TAN”, “ATN”, “PEEK”, “LEN”) duplicate earlier functions and drop
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] the mandatory  on MID, so they’d need cleaning up if you want a strict token list.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] If the goal is to catalogue only what the interpreter actually converts to one-byte tokens, the
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] earlier 8-per-line JSON I posted is the complete, documented set.
2025-12-09 07:34:13 [kimi-k2-0905] 
