2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • What is POS?*
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] `POS(X)` returns the horizontal cursor position (0–39). The argument `X` is a dummy value;
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] it is ignored but required.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • Vertical Position: There is no token for this. You are correct that you must read from
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] memory address 214: `PEEK(214)`.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • What is DEF?*
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] `DEF` is used to define a single-line user function (usually mathematical). It must be paired with
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] `FN`.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • Syntax Requirement: In C64 BASIC, a defined function must accept exactly one
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] numeric argument, even if you don't use it. The name must also start with `FN`.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • Your Example: `DEF ROW() = PEEK(214)` is invalid because of the missing argument and name
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] prefix.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • Corrected Example: `DEF FNR(X) = PEEK(214)` (Usage: `PRINT FNR(0)`).
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • No ELSE in Commodore BASIC 2?*
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] Correct. There is no `ELSE` token. You must use `GOTO` or reverse logic to handle conditionals.
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:35:53 [gemini-3-pro] • Example:*
