2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] How USR works
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] `USR(x)` allows BASIC to pass a value to a machine language subroutine.
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Setup: You must first write the starting address of your machine code routine (Low
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] Byte/High Byte) into memory locations 785 and 786 (0311–0312).
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Execution: When you call `Y = USR(X)`, BASIC evaluates `X`, stores it in Floating Point
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] Accumulator #1, and jumps to the address defined in 785/786.
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Return: The machine code routine executes. If it modifies the Floating Point
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] Accumulator, that value is returned to `Y` when the routine ends with an `RTS` (Return from
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] Subroutine).
2025-12-09 13:41:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:41:50 [nitro@ryzen5] really?
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, these are standard Commodore 64 BASIC v2 tokens.
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Token Allocation
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Program Flow
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Linear
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • `RUN`, `END`, `STOP`, `CONT`, `NEW`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • `GOTO`, `ON` (Computed GOTO), `IF`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Subroutines
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • `GOSUB`, `RETURN`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • `USR` (Machine language subroutine)
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Loops
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • `FOR`, `TO`, `NEXT`, `STEP`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Math
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • Arithmetic: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `^`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • Functions: `ABS`, `ATN`, `COS`, `EXP`, `INT`, `LOG`, `RND`, `SGN`, `SIN`, `SQR`, `TAN`
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] Clarifications
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • POS: This returns the horizontal cursor column (0-39). For vertical row, you are
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] correct; `PEEK(214)` is the standard method.
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • DEF: Used to define a user function (e.g., `DEF FNA(X) = X*X`). It must be followed by
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] `FN` when called.
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] • ELSE: Correct, BASIC v2 does not support `ELSE`.
