2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] How USR works
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] `USR(x)` is a bridge between BASIC and Machine Language (Assembly).
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Setup: You must `POKE` the starting address of your machine code routine into memory
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] locations 785 (low byte) and 786 (high byte).
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Execution: When BASIC encounters `X = USR(5)`, it evaluates the argument (`5`), places
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] that value into Floating Point Accumulator #1 (FAC1), and jumps to the address defined in 785/786.
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Return: The machine code performs its task. If it modifies the value in FAC1 and
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] returns (via `RTS`), that value is returned to the BASIC variable `X`.
2025-12-09 13:42:21 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-09 13:43:01 [nitro@ryzen5] hi kimi, can you help with the USR explanation in c64
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] USR lets BASIC call your own machine-code routine and get a numeric result back.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Put the routine’s start address (16-bit) in addresses 785/786 (0311–0312).
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905]    Low byte → 785, high byte → 786.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. From BASIC, call `result = USR(argument)`.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905]    - `argument` is evaluated and left in the C-64 floating-point accumulator (FAC #1).
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905]    - USR jumps to the address you stored.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Your routine can read or replace the value in FAC #1.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905]    End with RTS. Whatever value is in FAC #1 when you RTS becomes the function’s return value.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s all—no other setup required.
2025-12-09 13:43:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:35 [nitro@ryzen5] c64 has no floationg point?
2025-12-09 13:43:40 [kimi-k2-0905] It does—BASIC is built around a 5-byte floating-point format (1-byte exponent + 4-byte mantissa,
2025-12-09 13:43:40 [kimi-k2-0905] signed).
2025-12-09 13:43:40 [kimi-k2-0905] USR passes its argument in FAC #1 exactly that way; your machine-code can treat it as raw bytes or
2025-12-09 13:43:40 [kimi-k2-0905] call the built-in ROM routines (e.g., $BC0C to fetch/convert).
2025-12-09 13:43:40 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:43:54 [nitro@ryzen5] ohok no wonder it is so slow
2025-12-09 13:43:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Exactly—every numeric constant, variable, and operator in BASIC goes through that 5-byte float
2025-12-09 13:43:57 [kimi-k2-0905] package, so even simple integer loops pay the overhead.
2025-12-09 13:43:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:46:39 [nitro@ryzen5] the c64 load command in detail pls
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] LOAD — bring a program or data from tape/disk into RAM.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Syntax
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] LOAD ["filename"] [,device] [,relocate-flag]
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Token value: 147 ($93)
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Parameter details
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - filename: string constant or variable; if omitted the first file found is used.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - device: 1 = cassette, 8-11 = floppy (8 is default), other numbers are user-defined IEEE-488
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] devices.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - relocate-flag: 0 (or omitted) = load to the address stored in the file header; 1 = force load to
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] BASIC start regardless of header address (used for machine-code blocks).
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Execution flow
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. BASIC evaluates filename/device/flag.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Kernel jumps through indirect vectors at 0330/0332 so users can patch.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Device 1: turns on motor, reads header, compares filename if given, beeps on mismatch, loads
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] data, turns motor off.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Device 8+: sends TALK/TKSA on serial bus, receives 2-byte load address, then data; verifies
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] checksum per byte.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. On success:
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905]    - device 1 → sets ST=0, returns to direct mode.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905]    - device 8+ → sets ST=0, returns to direct mode.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905]    On error sets ST (6502 status byte) and prints "?FILE NOT FOUND", "?LOAD ERROR", etc.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Memory effects
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - Tape header is 192 bytes ($40) containing filename, start/end addresses, flag byte.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - LOAD always overwrites memory from the file’s start address upward; it does not relocate BASIC
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] pointers unless the load address lies inside BASIC program space.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] - If relocate-flag=1 the file is copied to BASIC start ($0801) regardless of header address, but
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] the kernel still expects the first two bytes of the file to be the original header address (they
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] are skipped).
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Return
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] No value is returned; control passes back to the READY prompt. A failed LOAD leaves memory
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] unchanged except for the I/O buffers used.
2025-12-09 13:47:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:14 [nitro@ryzen5] save commands
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] SAVE — write RAM to tape or disk.
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] Syntax
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] SAVE ["filename"] [,device [,start-addr [,end-addr+1]]]
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] Token value: 148 ($94)
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] Parameters
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - filename: string; if omitted cassette gets an empty name.
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - device: 1 = cassette, 8-11 = floppy (8 default).
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - start-addr (optional): 16-bit address where saving begins; default = start-of-BASIC ($0801).
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] - end-addr+1 (optional): 16-bit address of first byte NOT saved; default = end-of-BASIC pointers
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] (thus whole program).
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:32 [kimi-k2-0905] Execution
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. BASIC evaluates arguments.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Kernel uses indirect vectors 0334/0336 (patchable).
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Device 1: writes 192-byte header (type 01 for BASIC, 03 for raw data), then data, then short EOT
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] trailer; turns motor off.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Device 8+: sends TALK/TKSA, transmits 2-byte start, 2-byte end, data block, checksum.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. On success ST=0; on error ST non-zero and message "?SAVE ERROR" etc.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] Memory notes
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - SAVE never alters RAM contents.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - For machine-code saves you normally supply both addresses:
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905]   `SAVE "ML",8,8192,8320` saves 128 bytes starting at $2000.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - Cassette header flag byte: 01 BASIC, 03 SEQ/data, 04 PRG, 05 USR.
2025-12-09 13:49:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:05 [nitro@ryzen5] and same for verify please
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] VERIFY — compare RAM against the file just saved/loaded without changing memory.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Syntax
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] VERIFY ["filename"] [,device] [,relocate-flag]
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Token value: 149 ($95)
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Parameters
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - filename: string constant/variable; if omitted first file found is used.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - device: 1 cassette, 8-11 floppy (8 default).
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - relocate-flag: 0 (or omitted) = use header address; 1 = compare against BASIC start ($0801)
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] regardless of header.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Execution
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Kernel opens the device and fetches the file exactly like LOAD, but every incoming byte
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] is compared with the corresponding RAM byte instead of being written.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. If any byte differs, the routine stops immediately, sets ST to 10 ($0A) and prints "?VERIFY
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ERROR".
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. On perfect match ST remains 0 and BASIC returns to READY prompt.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Indirect vectors used: same as LOAD (0330/0332) so patches affect VERIFY too.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Notes
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - Cassette: motor control, header search and beeps are identical to LOAD.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - Disk: serial bus transaction is identical to LOAD except the byte is read then discarded after
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] comparison.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - No RAM is modified; pointers stay untouched.
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] - Common use: right after SAVE to be sure the media wrote correctly:
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905]   `SAVE "MYPROG",8: VERIFY "MYPROG",8`
2025-12-09 13:52:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:54:20 [nitro@ryzen5] vice netplay
2025-12-09 13:54:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Got it. Let me know if you need anything.
2025-12-09 13:54:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 13:54:35 [nitro@ryzen5] what is netplay vice c64 feature?
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Netplay in VICE (the Versatile Commodore Emulator) lets two or more people run the same C64 (or
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] C128, VIC-20, etc.) session over the network so that every joystick / keyboard action one player
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] makes is duplicated on the other machine in real time.
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Think of it as “remote joystick pass-through”: both sides see the identical screen, hear the same
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] audio, and stay cycle-exact because only the controller data is sent, not video or audio.
2025-12-09 13:54:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:02:41 [nitro@ryzen5] c64 vice and the ethernet connection
2025-12-09 14:02:45 [kimi-k2-0905] VICE’s netplay feature is UDP-only; it doesn’t care whether the packets go over Ethernet, Wi-Fi, or
2025-12-09 14:02:45 [kimi-k2-0905] a VLAN—just give it a reachable IP (or hostname) and make sure the single port you choose (default
2025-12-09 14:02:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 8081) is open in any firewall between the two machines.
2025-12-09 14:02:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:02:54 [nitro@ryzen5] not netplay
2025-12-09 14:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] If you’re not talking about netplay, the only other “Ethernet” thing VICE supports is the
2025-12-09 14:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] RR-Net/MMC64 cartridge emulation (CS8900A chip).
2025-12-09 14:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] That gives the C64 a real 10 Mbit interface inside the emulator; you can then run Contiki,
2025-12-09 14:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] WarpCopy, IBBS, etc., exactly like on a real C64 with an RR-Net cart.
2025-12-09 14:02:59 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:03:28 [nitro@ryzen5] what about a plain serial port passthrough?
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] VICE can hand the emulated CIA-A user-port lines straight to a real host serial port (or USB-serial
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] dongle) so the C64 “sees” whatever is electrically on those pins.
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] Use:
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] -serial1 /dev/ttyUSB0 -rs232user
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] (or COM3, etc.).
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] Baud rate, hand-shaking and the 5 V/-12 V levels are your problem—VICE just bit-bangs the data.
2025-12-09 14:03:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:06:11 [nitro@ryzen5] what else can it map it to?
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] Besides a real RS-232 device, VICE can map the user-port serial stream to:
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] - A TCP socket (client or server)
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905]   `-rs232userip 192.168.1.5:25232`
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905]   `-rs232userip 0.0.0.0:25232` (listen)
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] - A plain file (record or playback)
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905]   `-rs232userfile /tmp/c64.log`
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] - Another running VICE instance on the same PC (pipes)
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905]   `-rs232userpipe /tmp/vice.pipe`
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing else—those four targets cover every backend the emulator ships with.
2025-12-09 14:06:19 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:07:37 [nitro@ryzen5] C:\nitrologic\dsptool>C:\nitrologic\SDL2VICE-3.9-win64-r45883\x64sc.exe -rs232userip 0.0.0.0:25232 Detecting DLL based HardSID boards.Cannot open hardsid.dll.LoadLibrary WPCAP.DLL failed!EthernetARCH:  LoadLibrary WPCAP.DLL failed!Detecting PCI CatWeasel boards.No PCI CatWeasel found.Reading configuration file `C:\Users\nitro\AppData\Roaming\vice\sdl-vice.ini'.Unknown option '-rs232userip'.Error parsing command-line options, bailing out. For help use '-help'C:\nitrologic\dsptool>
