2025-12-09 14:21:58 [nitro@ryzen5] i have vice checkout out on my windows machine, can i rebuild with rs232 userr stuff? how?
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—MSYS2 is the path of least resistance on Windows.
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Install MSYS2 (https://www.msys2.org) and update it once:
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    `pacman -Syu`  (close the window when told, reopen, run again).
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Install the toolchain and deps:
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    pacman -S base-devel mingw-w64-x86_64-toolchain \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]              mingw-w64-x86_64-SDL2 \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]              mingw-w64-x86_64-gtk3 \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]              mingw-w64-x86_64-ffmpeg \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]              mingw-w64-x86_64-libpcap
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Open “MSYS2 MinGW 64-bit” shell, cd to your VICE source tree.
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Configure with user-port RS-232 enabled:
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    ./autogen.sh
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    ./configure --enable-rs232user --enable-rs232userip \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]                --with-pcap \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]                --with-fastsid \
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]                --enable-sdlui2
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 5. Build:
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    make -j$(nproc)
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 6. The fresh EXE ends up in
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    `src/arch/sdl/x64sc.exe`
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905]    (and siblings). Copy them anywhere; the supporting DLLs are already on PATH via MSYS2.
2025-12-09 14:22:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:23:15 [nitro@ryzen5] yeh nah, how about a windows distro?
2025-12-09 14:23:21 [kimi-k2-0905] There isn’t an “official” Windows distro with rs232-user pre-enabled, but the nightly builds on
2025-12-09 14:23:21 [kimi-k2-0905] https://buildbot.libretro.org/nightly/windows/x86_64/ (look for the vice/ folder) are compiled with
2025-12-09 14:23:21 [kimi-k2-0905] every switch turned on, including `--enable-rs232user` and `--enable-rs232userip`. Grab the newest
2025-12-09 14:23:21 [kimi-k2-0905] `-vice.zip`, unpack, and the contained `x64sc.exe` already understands `-rs232userip`.
2025-12-09 14:23:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:04 [nitro@ryzen5] was * = $0801 supported and could it be indented?
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—VICE’s built-in monitor accepts the same syntax:
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] * = $0801
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] and you can indent the line (spaces or tabs) if you like; the parser simply skips leading
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] white-space.
2025-12-09 14:48:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:48:33 [nitro@ryzen5] vice monitor key again
2025-12-09 14:48:34 [kimi-k2-0905] Alt+H
2025-12-09 14:48:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:49:14 [nitro@ryzen5] what do i type inside (C:$e5cf)
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] That prompt is the built-in machine-language monitor.
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Most-used one-letter commands:
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] g          ; run (Go) from current PC
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] g 1400     ; run from $1400
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] x          ; exit monitor, resume BASIC
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] m 0800 0fff ; memory dump $0800–$0FFF
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] d 0800     ; disassemble from $0800
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] s 0800 "HELLO" ; set ASCII at $0800
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] r          ; show registers
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] pc 1400    ; set PC to $1400
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] ;          ; assemble from current PC (then type 0800  LDA #$00 ...)
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Type `?` for full list.
2025-12-09 14:49:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:35 [nitro@ryzen5] how does ,y indexing work?
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] `,Y` adds the current Y-register value to the effective address after any 16-bit base
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] address is calculated.
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] That lets you walk through tables, strings, or screen rows with a simple `INY`.
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] Examples (all use zero-page or absolute base addresses):
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] LDA $F0,Y      ; read from $00F0+Y
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] STA $0400,Y    ; store into screen row 0 + Y
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] LDA ($FB),Y    ; indirect: take 16-bit pointer at $FB/$FC, then add Y
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] The CPU costs 1 extra cycle if the low-byte of the computed address crosses a page boundary (except
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] for the indirect form, which always costs the extra cycle).
2025-12-09 14:57:50 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:58:12 [nitro@ryzen5] is there pc relative + index?
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] The 6502 only has one program-counter-relative addressing mode—"relative" itself—and it is
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] only used by the eight branch instructions (`BCC`, `BEQ`, etc.). Those branches encode a
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] signed 8-bit displacement that is added to `PC+2`; there is no second index register involved and
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] no way to apply `,X` or `,Y` to the calculation.
2025-12-09 14:58:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:58:51 [nitro@ryzen5] are there common zero page address for doing user stuff?
2025-12-09 14:58:52 [nitro@ryzen5] start	ldx	#0loop	txa      pha      lda	message,y	beq	done	jsr	$ffd2      pla      tax	inx	bne	loopdone	pla      rtsmessage .byte "HELLO",13,0
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—C64 KERNAL and BASIC leave several bytes free.
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] The safest scratch area is FB–FE (four bytes); BASIC never touches them and the KERNAL only uses
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] them during serial-IEC ops you’re not running here.
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] So:
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrLo	= $FB
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrHi	= $FC
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] tmp1	= $FD
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] tmp2	= $FE
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Store your temporary pointer or counters there; keep FB/FC together if you need a 16-bit value.
2025-12-09 14:59:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:27 [nitro@ryzen5] can you do equates in c64 assembler?
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—every 6502 assembler accepts equates; the keyword differs:
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] TMP   = $FB        ; CBM prg Studio, 64tass, acme, ca65
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] PTR   .equ $FC     ; some older sources
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] After that `LDA TMP,X` is exactly `LDA $FB,X`.
2025-12-09 14:59:34 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:13 [nitro@ryzen5] is this valid? in c64
2025-12-09 15:01:15 [nitro@ryzen5] ; loop1.asm; assembler by simon; sys 2049 to run* = $0801	ptrLo = $FBptrHi = $FCtmp1 = $FDtmp2 = $FEstart	ldx	#0
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] Almost—BASIC’s start-up stub is missing, so if you load it as a PRG and type RUN you’ll crash. Add
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] the 12-byte header BASIC expects:
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] ; loop1.asm   SYS 2064 to run
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] * = $0801
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.word stubend  ; next line
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.word 2064     ; 2064 = $0810
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.byte $9e      ; SYS token
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 	.byte $32,$30,$36,$34,$00 ; "2064",0
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] stubend	.word 0        ; end of BASIC
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrLo = $FB
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrHi = $FC
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] tmp1  = $FD
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] tmp2  = $FE
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] start	ldx #0
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] Assemble, load, and either RUN or `SYS 2064`.
2025-12-09 15:01:31 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:02:26 [nitro@ryzen5] 2049 is still approp from sys call?
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 0801 is 2049, but the tiny BASIC stub itself sits at 0801-$080C, so the first byte of your
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] real code has to start after that.
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] In the snippet above the stub ends at 080C, so `start` is assembled at 080D (2061).
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a round number just move the stub one byte higher and `SYS 2064` (or any address you
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] like) as shown—change the line in the stub and the SYS argument to match.
2025-12-09 15:02:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:12:49 [nitro@ryzen5] js test string if it contains "="
2025-12-09 15:12:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 15:12:51 [kimi-k2-0905] function hasEquals(str) {
2025-12-09 15:12:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return str.includes("=");
2025-12-09 15:12:51 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-09 15:12:51 [kimi-k2-0905] 
