2025-12-09 16:17:31 [nitro@ryzen5] is that standard low high byte for which assemblers?
2025-12-09 16:17:35 [kimi-k2-0905] The `<` and `>` operators are understood by every 6502 cross-assembler in common use—64tass, ACME,
2025-12-09 16:17:35 [kimi-k2-0905] ca65, DASM, Ophis, CBM prg Studio, etc.—so the code above assembles anywhere without change.
2025-12-09 16:17:35 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:50 [nitro@ryzen5] an assembler on c64 that could assemble that file
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] Turbo Assembler (Turbo Assembler 5.2 or the later “Turbo Macro Pro”) running on the C64 itself
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] understands the identical syntax:
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrLo = $FB
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] ptrHi = $FC
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] start
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905]         lda #<message
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905]         sta ptrLo
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905]         lda #>message
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905]         sta ptrHi
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905]         ...
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] message .byte "HELLO",13,0
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] so you can feed the very same source file to tass (save it as SEQ or PRG) and assemble in-place
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] with A (assemble) and R (run).
2025-12-09 16:21:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:31:00 [nitro@ryzen5] c64 list first half of listing
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] OPEN 1,8,2,"LIST,S,R"
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] CMD 1
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] LIST
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] PRINT#1
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] CLOSE 1
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:31:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:31:23 [nitro@ryzen5] qnother way?
2025-12-09 16:31:24 [kimi-k2-0905] `LIST 1-`
2025-12-09 16:31:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:32:15 [nitro@ryzen5] whats prg and seg? how do i list just prg
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] PRG  = ordinary BASIC/program file (first two bytes are load address, rest is raw data).
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] SEQ  = sequential text/data file (no load address, just bytes).
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] To list only PRG files in a directory:
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] OPEN 1,8,2,"$0:P*=P"   :rem “P” filter = PRG
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:32:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:33:14 [nitro@ryzen5] what are arguments for list?
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] `LIST` accepts one optional range:
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] - `LIST`          whole program
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] - `LIST 10-`      from line 10 onward
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] - `LIST -50`      up to line 50
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] - `LIST 10-50`    line 10 through 50
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] - `LIST 10`       single line 10
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s all—no other parameters.
2025-12-09 16:33:20 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:35:17 [nitro@ryzen5] can i copy a file from drive 9 to 8 using wild card?
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—use the DOS copy command with wild-cards exactly as on drive 8:
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] OPEN 15,9,15,"C0:*=D1:*" : CLOSE 15
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] copies every file whose name matches “*” from drive 9 (device 9) into the current partition of
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] drive 8 (device 8).
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Change the pattern to something like “HELLO*” if you want a narrower match.
2025-12-09 16:35:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:36:11 [nitro@ryzen5] example with hello*
2025-12-09 16:36:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:36:14 [kimi-k2-0905] OPEN 15,9,15,"C0:HELLO*=D1:HELLO*":CLOSE 15
2025-12-09 16:36:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-09 16:36:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:01 [nitro@ryzen5] import * as THREE from 'three';
2025-12-10 01:13:05 [nitro@ryzen5] what lang is this?
2025-12-10 01:13:07 [kimi-k2-0905] JavaScript – it's an ES-module import.
2025-12-10 01:13:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:20 [nitro@ryzen5] how do es modules work?
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] An ES module is just a JS file whose exports/imports are statically declared.
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Export something
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    // math.js
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Import it elsewhere
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    // main.js
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    import { add } from './math.js';
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Tell the browser/runtime it’s a module
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905]    <script type="module" src="main.js"></script>
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] The loader resolves paths, executes each file once, keeps live bindings, and enforces strict mode
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] automatically.
2025-12-10 01:13:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:14:07 [nitro@ryzen5] an exe that creates a windows terminal window with api to read and write
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Windows doesn’t give you a “real” console handle you can simply open like a file.
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] The usual pattern is:
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Create a hidden helper console process (your own EXE or just `cmd.exe`).
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Redirect its STDIN / STDOUT through anonymous pipes.
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Expose a tiny DLL / COM / named-pipe API so any caller can read and write those pipes.
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a self-contained C example that does exactly that.
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] Compile it to `slop-term.exe` and run it; it listens on TCP 8081 and forwards every line to the
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] hidden console, then streams everything the console prints back to the socket.
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] (You can swap the socket for a named pipe, shared memory, or anything else.)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] /*
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905]  *  slop-term.c   –  hidden console behind a TCP port
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905]  *  cl /O2 slop-term.c ws2_32.lib
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905]  */
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #define _WIN32_WINNT 0x0600
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <winsock2.h>
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <windows.h>
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <stdio.h>
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #include <stdlib.h>
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] #pragma comment(lib,"ws2_32.lib")
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] static HANDLE hChildStdInRd, hChildStdInWr,
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905]               hChildStdOutRd, hChildStdOutWr;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] static SOCKET listenSock;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] static BOOL create_hidden_console(void)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES sa = { sizeof(sa), NULL, TRUE };
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (!CreatePipe(&hChildStdOutRd, &hChildStdOutWr, &sa, 0))
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		return FALSE;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (!CreatePipe(&hChildStdInRd, &hChildStdInWr, &sa, 0))
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		return FALSE;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	SetHandleInformation(hChildStdOutRd, HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, 0);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	SetHandleInformation(hChildStdInWr,  HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, 0);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	STARTUPINFOA si = { sizeof(si) };
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	si.dwFlags   = STARTF_USESHOWWINDOW | STARTF_USESTDHANDLES;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	si.wShowWindow = SW_HIDE;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	si.hStdInput   = hChildStdInRd;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	si.hStdOutput  = hChildStdOutWr;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	si.hStdError   = hChildStdOutWr;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	PROCESS_INFORMATION pi;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (!CreateProcessA("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", NULL, NULL, NULL,
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	                    TRUE, CREATE_NEW_CONSOLE, NULL, NULL, &si, &pi))
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		return FALSE;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	CloseHandle(pi.hThread);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	CloseHandle(pi.hProcess);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return TRUE;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] static DWORD WINAPI reader_thread(LPVOID clientSocket)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	SOCKET s = (SOCKET)clientSocket;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	char buf[4096];
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	DWORD nr;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	while (ReadFile(hChildStdOutRd, buf, sizeof(buf), &nr, NULL) && nr) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		send(s, buf, (int)nr, 0);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	closesocket(s);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return 0;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] static void handle_client(SOCKET s)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	CreateThread(NULL, 0, reader_thread, (LPVOID)s, 0, NULL);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	char buf[4096];
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	int  nr;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	while ((nr = recv(s, buf, sizeof(buf), 0)) > 0) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		DWORD nw, written = 0;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		while (written < (DWORD)nr) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 			if (!WriteFile(hChildStdInWr, buf + written,
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 			               nr - written, &nw, NULL))
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 				break;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 			written += nw;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	closesocket(s);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] int main(void)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	WSADATA wsd;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2,2), &wsd);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (!create_hidden_console()) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		printf("create_hidden_console failed %lu\n", GetLastError());
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		return 1;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	listenSock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	struct sockaddr_in addr = {0};
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	addr.sin_family      = AF_INET;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	addr.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(INADDR_LOOPBACK);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	addr.sin_port        = htons(8081);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (bind(listenSock, (struct sockaddr*)&addr, sizeof(addr)) == SOCKET_ERROR ||
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	    listen(listenSock, 4) == SOCKET_ERROR) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		printf("bind/listen failed %d\n", WSAGetLastError());
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		return 1;
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	printf("slop listening on 127.0.0.1:8081\n");
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	while (1) {
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		SOCKET client = accept(listenSock, NULL, NULL);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 		if (client != INVALID_SOCKET)
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 			handle_client(client);
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-10 06:15:26 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return 0;
