2025-12-10 08:13:56 [nitro@ryzen5] in 6502 can i load a form indirect zero page?
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—opcode `(d),Y` does it.
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Example:
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] LDY #4       ; index
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] LDA ($FB),Y  ; loads from address held in $FB/$FC plus Y
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Zero-page address in `FB` (low) / `FC` (high), 16-bit pointer plus Y gives final effective address.
2025-12-10 08:14:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:14:55 [nitro@ryzen5] ldy from zero page?
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—immediate and zero-page addressing modes both exist.
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] Immediate:
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] LDY #$05     ; Y ← 05
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] Zero-page:
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] LDY $42      ; Y ← contents of memory $0042
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] No “indirect-zero-page” form for LDY itself; only the pointer forms like `(d),Y` apply to
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] LDA/STA/etc.
2025-12-10 08:15:02 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:24 [nitro@ryzen5] zero page location of user index
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] C-64 KERNAL doesn’t reserve a dedicated “user index” byte, so you’re free to pick any unused
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] zero-page location. Common practice:
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - FB/FC (251/252): pointer scratch
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - FD/FE (253/254): pointer scratch
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] - A3–A7 (163–167): BASIC FP work area, safe if BASIC is off
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] For a single-byte user index, `$A3` is a popular choice.
2025-12-10 08:15:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:16:20 [nitro@ryzen5] store 0 in zero page?>
2025-12-10 08:16:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:16:24 [kimi-k2-0905] LDA #0
2025-12-10 08:16:24 [kimi-k2-0905] STA $A3   ; store 0 into zero-page location $A3
2025-12-10 08:16:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:16:24 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:18:13 [nitro@ryzen5] ```; hello2064.prg; a program to output "HELLO 2064"; commodore 64 assembly language; SYS($9e)2064 basic stub @ $801 (2049); p16 is a zero page 16 bit address char pointer ($fb,$fc); t0 is an 8 bit index for use with p16; CHROUT is kernal subroutine called, it does trash clobber burn A X Y* = $0801p16 = $fbt0 = $a3chrout = $FFD2; PET-BASIC stub SYS($9e)2064.word line2.word 2064.byte $9e,"2064",0line2.word 0start	lda #0	sta t0	lda #<hello	sta p16	lda #>hello	sta p16+1loop	ldy t0	lda (p16),y	beq done	jsr chrout	inc t0	bne loopdone	rtshello	.byte "HELLO 2064",13,0```
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Clean, works. Only two tiny nits:
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. CHROUT clobbers A, not X/Y, so your header comment is off.
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. You can drop the trailing `13` if you just want the string; BASIC will print the CR itself when
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] it returns.
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] Otherwise assemble and `SYS 2064` prints "HELLO 2064".
2025-12-10 08:18:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:41:25 [nitro@ryzen5] in vice c64 rs232 settings, what is ip232?
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] In VICE’s RS-232 settings “IP232” is the built-in driver that lets the emulated C-64 user-port
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] RS-232 talk to a real TCP socket instead of a host serial port.
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] You give it an IP address and port (e.g. 192.168.1.20:25232) and VICE opens that TCP connection;
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] every byte the C-64 sends on the user-port TX line goes out as TCP data, and every byte that
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] arrives on the socket is presented to the C-64 on the RX line—exactly what a real IP232 adaptor or
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] WiModem does.
2025-12-10 09:41:38 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:19 [nitro@ryzen5] simple c64 code to write hello world, and deno code to listen and accept on host
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] C64 side (1200 baud, 8N1, assumes UP2400/UP9600 or compatible cart for true 2400+):
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 10 OPEN2,2,3,CHR$(6)+CHR$(0)   :rem 2=rs232, 3=cmd, 6=2400
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 20 PRINT#2,"HELLO WORLD"
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 30 CLOSE2
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Deno side (listens on 8081, accepts one client, echoes everything back):
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] // host.ts
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] import { serve } from "https://deno.land/std@0.208.0/net/mod.ts";
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] const listener = Deno.listen({ port: 8081 });
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] console.log("IP232 listener on :8081");
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] for await (const conn of listener) {
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]   console.log("client connected");
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]   (async () => {
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]     try {
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]       await conn.readable.pipeTo(conn.writable);
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]     } catch (_) {} // client gone
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]     console.log("client gone");
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905]   })();
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Run:
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `deno run --allow-net host.ts`
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] In VICE → Settings → RS-232 → Device 2 → IP232 → Host `localhost`, Port `8081`.
2025-12-10 09:42:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
