2025-12-10 14:26:33 [nitro@ryzen5] exact behavior of
2025-12-10 14:26:33 [nitro@ryzen5] 			const line=raw.replace(/\s+/g, " ");
2025-12-10 14:26:42 [kimi-k2-0905] `raw.replace(/\s+/g, " ")` collapses every run of any Unicode white-space character
2025-12-10 14:26:42 [kimi-k2-0905] (spaces, tabs, new-lines, NBSP, etc.) into a single ASCII space.
2025-12-10 14:26:42 [kimi-k2-0905] Leading and trailing runs are also reduced to one space, so the result never starts or ends with
2025-12-10 14:26:42 [kimi-k2-0905] white-space, and internal runs become exactly one space.
2025-12-10 14:26:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 14:27:41 [nitro@ryzen5] Leading and trailing runs are also reduced to one space, so the result never starts or ends withwhite-space, and internal runs become exactly one space.
2025-12-10 14:27:42 [nitro@ryzen5] ?????
2025-12-10 14:27:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 14:27:45 [kimi-k2-0905] // one-liner
2025-12-10 14:27:45 [kimi-k2-0905] const line = raw.trim().replace(/\s+/g, ' ');
2025-12-10 14:27:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 14:27:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-10 14:27:56 [nitro@ryzen5] please explain your logix
2025-12-10 14:28:00 [kimi-k2-0905] `trim()` first strips leading/trailing whitespace, then the regex collapses whatever is
2025-12-10 14:28:00 [kimi-k2-0905] left, so the final string can’t start or end with a space.
