2025-12-11 20:34:17 [nitro@ryzen5] i can't remember what i meant:
2025-12-11 20:34:17 [nitro@ryzen5] CSYNC based on fixed threshold, 2 gray and 1 white detector adjustable with trimmers.
2025-12-11 20:34:23 [kimi-k2-0905] You fixed the sync slice level with a resistor divider (≈ 0.3 V) so the comparator always trips on
2025-12-11 20:34:23 [kimi-k2-0905] sync pulses, while the two “grey” channels and the “white” channel each get their own 10 k trim-pot
2025-12-11 20:34:23 [kimi-k2-0905] so you can tweak where the brightness steps happen.
2025-12-11 20:34:23 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:34:52 [nitro@ryzen5] how about the wiring for counting chroma
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] Chroma sub-carrier (≈ 4.43 MHz PAL or 3.58 MHz NTSC) is AC-coupled to one spare LM319 comparator.
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] Pick an unused channel (e.g. IC3-B) and wire it like this:
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] RCA centre ──||──┬──┐
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905]          1 nF   │  │
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905]                 │  R 1 k (to ground, bias)
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905]                 │  │
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905]                 └──┴─ LM319 IN+ (pin 11)
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] LM319 IN- (pin 12) ── 10 k trimpot wiper (leg 1 → 3V3, leg 3 → GND)
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] LM319 OUT (pin 13) → Pico GP16 (internal pull-up on)
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] The comparator now delivers a 0-3V3 square wave at sub-carrier rate.
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] In the PIO program clock GP16 edges for ~280 ns after each H-sync; the 0/1/2/3 count gives the
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 2-bit chroma index you pack alongside the 2-bit luma.
2025-12-11 20:35:08 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:37:31 [nitro@ryzen5] list the individual connections
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] Chroma-only wiring (one extra LM319 channel)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 1 RCA jack centre pin
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 2 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 3 ├─ 1 nF ceramic (C7)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 4 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 5 ├─ 1 kΩ (R18) to GND  (bias / termination helper)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 6 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 7 └─ LM319-3 pin 11 (IN B+)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 8 LM319-3 pin 12 (IN B-)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 9 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 10 └─ 10 kΩ trimpot (R19) wiper
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 11 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 12 ├─ trimpot leg 1 → 3V3
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 13 └─ trimpot leg 3 → GND
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 14 LM319-3 pin 13 (OUT B)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 15 │
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 16 └─ Pico GP16 (set internal pull-up)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 17 LM319-3 pin 14 → 3V3
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 18 LM319-3 pin 1  → GND
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 19 100 nF (C8) across pins 14-1 (VCC-GND)
2025-12-11 20:37:45 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:41 [nitro@ryzen5] best way to have two separate projects (same sdk family) cpu
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] Give each project its own virtual environment (or conda env) that points at the same CPU-only SDK
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] build.
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing is shared except the runtime you installed once; the dependency trees stay isolated.
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] # 1. Install the SDK once, CPU build
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] python -m pip install "your-sdk[cpu]" -f https://download.your-sdk.com/whl/cpu
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] # 2. Make an env for project A
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] python -m venv ~/venvs/proj-a
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] source ~/venvs/proj-a/bin/activate
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] python -m pip install -e ~/src/proj-a          # editable install
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] # 3. Make an env for project B
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] python -m venv ~/venvs/proj-b
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] source ~/venvs/proj-b/bin/activate
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] python -m pip install -e ~/src/proj-b
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] Activate whichever environment you need; both use the same CPU runtime, nothing collides.
2025-12-12 12:14:55 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:06 [nitro@ryzen5] in cmake do i declare PICO_PLATFORM multipl times ?
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] No.
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] `PICO_PLATFORM` is a cache variable; set it once, before the first `project()` call, and every
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] target in that build tree inherits it.
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] # top-level CMakeLists.txt
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] set(PICO_PLATFORM rp2040 CACHE STRING "")
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] project(my_pico_app C CXX ASM)
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] # …add_subdirectory(pico-sdk) etc.
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] If you need two different platforms you need two separate build trees (e.g. `build-rp2040`,
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] `build-rp2350`) and configure each with its own `-DPICO_PLATFORM=…`.
2025-12-12 12:16:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-12 13:15:59 [nitro@ryzen5] ok 2350 gets its own dir and lists txt
