2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] If you’re doing zero-crossing detection on the chroma subcarrier, the hardware goal is:
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] produce a clean 3V3 digital square wave whose transitions line up with chroma zero crossings, then
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] have the PIO sample that wave at a fixed phase relative to each pixel.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] C64 timing anchors (useful numbers)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Pixel rate (approx)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- PAL C64: ~7.88 MHz pixel clock equivalent (63 µs line / ~504 “pixels”)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- NTSC C64: ~8.18 MHz equivalent
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Chroma subcarrier
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- PAL: 4.43361875 MHz (period ~226 ns)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- NTSC: 3.579545 MHz (period ~279 ns)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] So your earlier “~280 ns window” matches about one NTSC chroma cycle, but is longer than
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] one PAL cycle.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] What the PIO can realistically do per pixel
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] Trying to “count Hz” inside a tiny ~1 pixel window is hard because:
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - One pixel is ~122–127 ns (for ~8 MHz), which is shorter than a chroma cycle (226–279 ns).
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - So per pixel you can’t reliably count full cycles; instead you usually:
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	1) Track phase of chroma by sampling the zero-crossing square wave at a known time, or
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	2) Measure chroma phase over multiple pixels / a burst reference and then increment a phase
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] accumulator.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] Hardware wiring that best supports zero-crossing
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Make the chroma comparator a zero-crossing slicer:
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- AC-couple chroma through a capacitor into a node biased at mid-rail (≈1.65 V).
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- Feed that node to LM319 + input.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- Set LM319 – input to the same mid-rail reference (or a tiny offset if you want
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] hysteresis with feedback).
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Add pull-up on LM319 output to 3V3 (4.7 k is fine).
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Consider adding a little hysteresis (positive feedback resistor) because chroma is small
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] and noisy; without it you’ll get extra transitions.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] Key question before “PIO instructions per pixel”
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] How are you defining “color of each pixel”?
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Option A: sample chroma phase at the pixel sample time (common approach)
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- PIO waits for luma sampling point, then reads the chroma square wave (0/1).
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- Over time you reconstruct phase by comparing to a line/burst reference.
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Option B: count edges in a fixed window
2025-12-12 17:34:19 [gpt-5.2-2025] 	- Works better as a line-level lock/check (is chroma present? PAL vs NTSC?), not really
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] per pixel.
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] To talk PIO clocks/instructions sensibly, I need:
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] - RP2040 sys_clk you’ll run (125 MHz default, or overclock?).
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Desired pixel output width: you mention 320 pixels; are you resampling horizontally (C64 ~504-ish
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] samples/line) down to 320?
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Do you have a stable reference point each line (CSYNC edge) to start a phase schedule?
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] If you answer those three, I can suggest a practical scheme like:
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] - One PIO SM clocked at N× pixel rate to sample (G0..G2 + chroma bit) at deterministic times.
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] - Another SM (or DMA pacing) to align to HSYNC and generate per-line capture windows.
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] 
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] And we can decide whether “zero-crossing chroma” gives you enough info for 2-bit chroma
2025-12-12 17:34:20 [gpt-5.2-2025] index, or whether you’ll want a burst-locked phase accumulator instead.
