2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] That’s a KA319 (often a Samsung or Fairchild second-source part). It’s functionally
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] equivalent to the LM319, but you should check its datasheet for minimum supply voltage.
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] Most LM319/KA319 variants are 5 V minimum for guaranteed operation. Some may work down to
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 3.3 V, but they’re not characterized there, so performance (speed, input range, output saturation)
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] is not guaranteed.
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] Given you’re already planning to run them at 5 V, that’s fine. Just ensure:
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] - VCC = 5 V
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pull-ups to 3.3 V only (Pico internal or external 4.7 kΩ to 3.3 V)
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] - Grounds tied together (RCA shield, LM319 GND, Pico GND)
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] - Decoupling caps (100 nF) at each LM319 VCC/GND pins
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] If you later want to move to a 3.3 V comparator for simplicity, look for parts like
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] TLV3201, MAX902, or LTC1540 that are specified for 3.3 V and have
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] open-drain outputs.
2025-12-12 18:13:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:05 [nitro@ryzen5] i have waveshare display with touch and it says XPT2046?
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] Yes, the XPT2046 is a common 4‑wire resistive touch controller used on many Waveshare LCDs
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] (including the 2‑inch Pico LCD). It’s an SPI‑based chip that provides touch‑screen coordinates.
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] If your display has touch, the XPT2046 will typically share the LCD’s SPI bus (CLK, MOSI, MISO) but
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] use a separate CS (chip‑select) pin. You’ll need to check the Waveshare pinout for your
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] exact model to see which GPIO is assigned to touch CS.
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] Common assignments on Waveshare “Pico LCD 2” with touch:
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - LCD_CS → GP9
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - TOUCH_CS → GP16 (or sometimes GP22)
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - SPI CLK → GP10 (shared)
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - SPI MOSI → GP11 (shared)
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - SPI MISO → GP12 or GP8 (shared for touch data back)
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] If you’re using GP16 for Chroma in your LUMA2 design, that would conflict. You’d need to either:
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - Remap Chroma to another free GPIO (check what’s left after LCD + touch), or
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] - Disable touch if you don’t need it (leave touch CS unconnected).
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] Want me to look up the exact pinout for your specific Waveshare product code?
2025-12-12 18:18:17 [deepseek-chat] 
