2025-12-15 14:40:48 [nitro@ryzen5]  ineed tyo catch invalidstate in promise
2025-12-15 14:40:50 [nitro@ryzen5] 			port.open({baudRate:115200,flowControl:"none"}).then(function(){				try{					console.log("Port open "+that.name);							that.closed=false;					that.check();				}catch(e){					log("Port open exception "+e);				}						});		}catch(e){			log("connectSerialPort exception "+e);		}
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] You have a `port.open()` promise that needs error handling for invalid state. The issue is that the
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] `.then()` only handles success, but you also need `.catch()` for promise rejections.
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] Here's the corrected structure:
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] port.open({ baudRate: 115200, flowControl: "none" })
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   .then(function() {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]     try {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]       console.log("Port open " + that.name);
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]       that.closed = false;
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]       that.check();
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]     } catch(e) {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]       log("Port open exception " + e);
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]     }
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   })
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   .catch(function(e) {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]     log("Port open promise rejection " + e);
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]     // Handle invalid state or other promise rejections here
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   });
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] Key changes:
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 1. Added `.catch()` after `.then()` to handle promise rejections
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 2. The `.catch()` will handle `InvalidStateError` and other promise rejections
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 3. The inner `try-catch` only handles synchronous errors in the success callback
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] Alternatively, you can use async/await with try-catch:
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] try {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   await port.open({ baudRate: 115200, flowControl: "none" });
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   console.log("Port open " + that.name);
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   that.closed = false;
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   that.check();
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] } catch(e) {
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat]   log("Port open error " + e);
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-15 14:41:01 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:29 [nitro@ryzen5] for js objects does for in iterate keys or valures?
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] For `for...in` loops in JavaScript, it iterates over the keys (property names) of an
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] object.
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] Example:
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] const obj = { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 };
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] for (const key in obj) {
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(key); // Outputs: "a", "b", "c"
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat]   console.log(obj[key]); // Access the value
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] Note: It iterates over enumerable properties, including inherited ones. Use `hasOwnProperty()` to
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] filter out inherited properties.
2025-12-16 12:27:35 [deepseek-chat] 
