2025-12-16 15:24:40 [nitro@ryzen5] how to sample pin with pot x old school paddle
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] For sampling an analog pin with a potentiometer (old-school paddle style):
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] • Arduino/ESP32 Example:*
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] const int paddlePin = A0;  // Analog pin
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] int paddleValue = 0;
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] void setup() {
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.begin(115200);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, INPUT);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] void loop() {
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	paddleValue = analogRead(paddlePin);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	// Map to screen position: 0-1023 to 0-255
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	int screenPos = map(paddleValue, 0, 1023, 0, 255);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.println(screenPos);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	delay(10);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] • JavaScript Web Serial Reading:*
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] // In your Port.read() method, parse the incoming data
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] if(value) {
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	var text = new TextDecoder().decode(value);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	var lines = text.split('\n');
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	lines.forEach(line => {
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 		if(line.trim()) {
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 			var paddlePos = parseInt(line.trim());
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 			// Update paddle position on screen
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 			updatePaddle(paddlePos);
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 		}
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 	});
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] The potentiometer acts as a voltage divider, giving 0-5V (0-1023 on 10-bit ADC) that you map to
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] screen coordinates.
2025-12-16 15:24:51 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:25:55 [nitro@ryzen5] you put 5v in one side and measure the other? i mean 3v
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] Yes, exactly. For 3.3V systems (like ESP32):
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] • Wiring:*
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ──┬── Potentiometer Pin 1
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat]        │
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat]        ├── Wiper (middle pin) ──→ ADC Pin (A0)
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat]        │
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] GND ───┴── Potentiometer Pin 3
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] • Code adjustment:*
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] paddleValue = analogRead(paddlePin);  // Returns 0-4095 (12-bit on ESP32)
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] int screenPos = map(paddleValue, 0, 4095, 0, 255);
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] The wiper voltage varies from 0V to 3.3V as you turn the knob, giving 0-4095 on ESP32's 12-bit ADC.
2025-12-16 15:26:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:29 [nitro@ryzen5] on pico vsys or vbus?
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] For Raspberry Pi Pico:
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] • Use `VBUS`* (pin 40) for 5V from USB.
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] • Use `VSYS`* (pin 39) for battery/regulated input (1.8V-5.5V).
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] For a simple paddle with potentiometer:
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] - Use `3V3(OUT)` (pin 36) for 3.3V to the pot
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] - ADC pins (26-28) are 3.3V max
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] - Pot wiper → ADC pin (GP26-28)
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] - Pot other pins → 3V3 and GND
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] • Example:*
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] #include <Arduino.h>
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] const int paddlePin = 26;  // ADC0 (GP26)
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] void setup() {
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.begin(115200);
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	analogReadResolution(12);  // 0-4095
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] void loop() {
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	int paddleValue = analogRead(paddlePin);
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	int screenPos = map(paddleValue, 0, 4095, 0, 255);
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.println(screenPos);
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 	delay(10);
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] • Wiring:*
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 3V3(OUT) pin 36 ── Pot pin 1
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] GP26 (ADC0) ────── Pot wiper (middle)
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] GND pin 38 ─────── Pot pin 3
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:26:41 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:18 [nitro@ryzen5] the paddle only has a single pin, maybe we use charging technique old school?
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] Ah, single-pin paddle! You're thinking of the capacitor charging method used in old
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] Atari/Commodore paddles.
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] • How it worked:*
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 1. Pin charges a capacitor through the paddle's potentiometer
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 2. Microcontroller times how long to discharge
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 3. Longer time = higher resistance = paddle position
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] • Pico Implementation:*
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] const int paddlePin = 15;  // Any digital pin
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] void setup() {
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, INPUT);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.begin(115200);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] int readPaddle() {
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, OUTPUT);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	digitalWrite(paddlePin, HIGH);  // Charge cap
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	delayMicroseconds(10);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, INPUT_PULLDOWN);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	unsigned long start = micros();
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	// Wait for pin to go LOW (cap discharged through pot)
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	while(digitalRead(paddlePin) == HIGH) {
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 		if(micros() - start > 10000) break;  // Timeout
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	}
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	unsigned long duration = micros() - start;
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	return constrain(duration, 0, 10000);  // 0-10000µs range
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] void loop() {
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	int paddleTime = readPaddle();
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	int screenPos = map(paddleTime, 0, 10000, 0, 255);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	Serial.println(screenPos);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 	delay(50);
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] • Wiring:*
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] Pico GPIO ───┬── 0.1µF capacitor ─── GND
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat]              │
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat]              └── Potentiometer ──── GND
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat]                   (single pin paddle)
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] The pot resistance (50kΩ-1MΩ) controls discharge time.
2025-12-16 15:29:34 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:31:51 [nitro@ryzen5] how about the analog pin method, that is noty possible with classic 9 pin connector?
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] You're right - classic 9-pin D-sub connectors (Atari/Commodore) didn't have analog pins. They used
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] digital pins only with the capacitor timing method.
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] • 9-pin D-sub pinout for paddles:*
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 1: Up
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 2: Down
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 3: Left
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 4: Right
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 5: Paddle B (button)
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 6: Button A (fire)
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 7: +5V
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 8: Ground
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] Pin 9: Paddle A (capacitor timing)
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] • How it worked:*
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pin 9: Capacitor discharge timing (paddle position)
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pin 5: Second paddle button
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pins 1-4: Joystick directions
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pin 6: Fire button
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pin 7: +5V (for potentiometer)
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] - Pin 8: Ground
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] So the capacitor method was mandatory - no analog pins available on the connector. The
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] computer measured discharge time through the pot to determine position.
2025-12-16 15:32:03 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:19 [nitro@ryzen5] 5v for pot? the method you descibed the pot pin was given charge
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] You're absolutely right - my capacitor method was wrong for the classic design. Let me correct:
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] • Classic Atari/Commodore paddle actually:*
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 1. Computer provides +5V (pin 7) to one side of pot
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 2. Pot wiper connects to timing pin (pin 9)
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 3. Computer charges a capacitor internally through the pot resistance
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 4. Measures charge time (not discharge)
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] • Correct method:*
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] int readPaddle() {
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, OUTPUT);
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	digitalWrite(paddlePin, LOW);  // Discharge cap first
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	delayMicroseconds(100);
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	// Switch to input and time how long to charge HIGH
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	pinMode(paddlePin, INPUT);
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	unsigned long start = micros();
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	while(digitalRead(paddlePin) == LOW) {
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 		if(micros() - start > 10000) break;
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	}
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 	return micros() - start;
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] }
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] • Wiring:*
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] +5V (pin 7) ─── Pot pin 1
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat]                Pot wiper ─── Timing pin (pin 9)
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] GND (pin 8) ─── Pot pin 3
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] The pot acts as a variable resistor controlling RC charge time. Higher resistance = slower charge =
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] different position.
2025-12-16 15:33:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 20:14:54 [nitro@ryzen5] when connecting CD4051 what is INH and VEE pins?
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] The CD4051 analog multiplexer/demultiplexer has the following pin functions:
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] • INH (Inhibit)* - When pulled HIGH (typically to VDD), this pin disables all channels.
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] When LOW, the device operates normally based on the address inputs.
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] • VEE* - This is the negative supply pin for analog signal handling. It allows the device
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] to process analog signals that swing below ground (negative voltages). For digital-only operation,
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] VEE can be tied to ground (0V). For analog signals that go negative, VEE should be connected to a
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] negative voltage (e.g., -5V).
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] Typical connections:
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] - VDD: +5V (digital supply)
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] - VSS: Ground (0V)
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] - VEE: 0V for digital/positive-only signals, or negative voltage for bipolar analog signals
2025-12-16 20:15:06 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 12:58:35 [nitro@ryzen5] both luma and chroma pins on a video port orf c64 will always be +ve?
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] No, both luma (Y) and chroma (C) signals on the C64's video port are composite video
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] signals that swing both positive and negative relative to ground.
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] Specifically:
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Luma (Y) carries brightness/black-and-white information
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Chroma (C) carries color information
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] Both are AC-coupled signals (typically through a capacitor) with:
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Positive-going sync pulses
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Negative-going video information
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Average DC level around 0V
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] For proper handling with a CD4051 or similar analog switch:
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - You would need to provide a negative VEE supply (e.g., -5V)
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Or use DC restoration/biasing circuits
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] - Or use a different analog switch that can handle bipolar signals with single supply
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] The C64's video output is designed to drive a 75Ω load to standard composite video levels (1Vpp).
2025-12-17 12:58:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 13:00:09 [nitro@ryzen5] i am expecting blanking voltage on luma pin to be betwen 0 and 0.32
2025-12-17 13:00:14 [nitro@ryzen5] volts
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] You're correct. On the C64's luma (Y) pin, during blanking intervals (horizontal
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] and vertical sync), the voltage is indeed low:
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - Active video: ~0.7V to 1.0V (white to black)
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - Blanking/sync: ~0V to 0.32V (sync tips at ~0V)
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] The sync pulses actually go slightly negative relative to the blanking level, but due to AC
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] coupling and the C64's specific output circuit, at the connector you'll measure:
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - Blanking level: ~0.3V
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - Sync tips: ~0V to 0.1V
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] So your expectation of 0V to 0.32V during blanking is accurate for measurements at the
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] video port.
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] For analog switching with a CD4051:
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - With VEE = 0V (ground), you can safely handle these positive-only voltages
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - No negative supply needed for just the luma signal
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] - Ensure VDD is at least +5V for proper switching
2025-12-17 13:00:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:06:37 [nitro@ryzen5] was Signetics 2650 a clone of another cpu?
2025-12-17 14:06:42 [deepseek-chat] The Signetics 2650 was not a clone of another CPU. It was an original 8-bit microprocessor design
2025-12-17 14:06:42 [deepseek-chat] introduced by Signetics in 1975. It had a unique architecture with features like a 15-bit program
2025-12-17 14:06:42 [deepseek-chat] counter, 8-bit data bus, and a relatively orthogonal instruction set. While it competed with other
2025-12-17 14:06:42 [deepseek-chat] 8-bit CPUs like the Intel 8080 and Motorola 6800, it was not a direct copy of any existing design.
2025-12-17 14:06:42 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:07:11 [nitro@ryzen5] what popular video games used it?
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] The Signetics 2650 was not widely used in mainstream arcade or home console games, but it did
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] appear in a few notable arcade titles in the late 1970s. The most famous game to use it was:
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] • Gorf (1981) – A popular multi-stage arcade shooter by Midway. The 2650 was used in the
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] arcade cabinet.
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] Other arcade games that used the Signetics 2650 include:
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] • Astro Fighter (1980)
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] • Space Fortress (1980)
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] • Professor Pac-Man (1983) – A rare educational variant.
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] It was not used in any major home consoles or computers, limiting its presence in the video game
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] world primarily to these niche arcade cabinets.
2025-12-17 14:07:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:14 [nitro@ryzen5] gorf was z80
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] You are absolutely correct. My apologies for the error. Thank you for the correction.
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] The arcade game Gorf (1981) used a Zilog Z80 CPU, not the Signetics 2650.
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] The Signetics 2650's use in video games was even more limited. The most well-known example is:
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] • Astro Fighter (1980) – A popular arcade shooter by Data East.
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] Other, more obscure arcade titles that used the 2650 include:
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] • Space Fortress (1980)
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] • Professor Pac-Man (1983)
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] • The Games: Summer Edition (1984)
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] It was not a mainstream gaming CPU and saw no use in major home systems. Its primary legacy is as
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] an interesting, unique, but commercially minor 8-bit architecture.
2025-12-17 14:10:22 [deepseek-chat] 
