2025-12-17 20:14:10 [nitro@ryzen5] and if i am only working with +ve signals I can connect it to ground?
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] Hello.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, regarding the KA319 (equivalent to the LM319), VEE is the negative supply voltage,
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] often referred to as V-.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding connecting VEE to ground:
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, you can connect VEE to ground for single-supply operation, but there is a specific limitation
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] to keep in mind. Unlike some other comparators (like the LM339), the KA319 inputs cannot go
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] all the way down to the negative supply rail.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] The input common-mode voltage range typically starts about 1.0V above VEE.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] • If your signals are strictly above 1V: Connecting VEE to Ground will work fine.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] • If your signals go down to 0V: The comparator will not function correctly. You would need
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] a small negative voltage on VEE or a rail-to-rail input comparator instead.
2025-12-17 20:14:28 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:15:34 [nitro@ryzen5] this is for sampling luma pin of c64video pin of c64
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] Hello.
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, on the KA319 (equivalent to LM319), V_EE represents the negative supply rail, often
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] labeled as V-.
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding your specific application for the C64 Luma signal:
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  VEE Connection: Yes, the KA319 is designed to operate on either dual supplies or a
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] single supply. If you are processing only positive signals, you can connect VEE (Pin 6)
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] directly to Ground.
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Grounding: Be sure to connect both the Analog Negative Supply (V_EE, Pin 6) and the
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] Logic Ground (GND, Pin 3) to your system ground.
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] Since the KA319 input common-mode voltage range includes the negative rail, this configuration
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] allows it to accurately sample the C64 Luma signal, which sits between 0V and roughly 1V relative
2025-12-17 20:15:53 [gemini-3-pro] to ground.
