2025-12-17 21:42:24 [nitro@ryzen5] what does jaycar mean by tinned copper wire?
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] Hello. I can certainly clarify those technical details for you.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] KA319 Pinout and Voltage
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Is V_EE the same as V-?
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro]     Yes. In the context of the KA319 (and its equivalent, the LM319), Pin 8 is labeled
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] V_EE, which represents the Negative Supply voltage.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Can you connect V_EE to Ground?
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro]     Yes, if you are working strictly with positive signals (Single Supply operation).
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro]     *   You should connect Pin 8 (V_EE) to Ground.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro]     *   You must also ensure Pin 11 (GND) is connected to Ground.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Context: The Commodore 64 Luma signal is a positive voltage video signal (typically
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 0V to +1V range), so a single supply configuration (0V and +5V or +12V) is generally suitable for
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] sampling this.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] Jaycar Tinned Copper Wire
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] When Jaycar (or any electronics supplier) refers to tinned copper wire, it means:
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] • Material: The core is solid or stranded copper.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] • Coating: The copper is plated with a thin layer of tin (silver in color).
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] • Purpose: The tin coating prevents the copper from oxidizing (turning green/black) and
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] makes the wire significantly easier to solder, as solder adheres to tin better than bare copper.
2025-12-17 21:42:48 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:28:10 [nitro@ryzen5] how does curl use sockets on linux?
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] `curl` (via the `libcurl` library) interacts with the Linux kernel's networking stack using
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] standard system calls to manage the lifecycle of a network connection.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the general flow of how it utilizes sockets:
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Resolution: It first resolves the domain name (DNS), often opening a temporary socket
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] to communicate with a DNS server.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Creation: It calls `socket()` to create a new endpoint for communication, which returns
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] a file descriptor.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Connection: It calls `connect()` to initiate the TCP 3-way handshake with the remote
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] server.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Multiplexing: It frequently uses `poll()` or `select()` to monitor the socket's status,
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] allowing it to wait for the connection to establish or for data to become available without
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] blocking the entire process.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  Transfer: Once connected, it uses `send()` (or `write()`) to transmit the HTTP request
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] and `recv()` (or `read()`) to ingest the response.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 6.  Teardown: Finally, it calls `close()` to terminate the connection and release the file
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] descriptor.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] If you would like a C code example demonstrating these specific system calls, please request it.
2025-12-20 07:28:24 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:28:53 [nitro@ryzen5] what lib is socket on?
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] • How curl uses sockets on Linux*
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] `curl` operates as a user-space client that interacts with the Linux kernel's networking subsystem
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] via system calls. The general process is:
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Resolution: It resolves the hostname to an IP address (often using `getaddrinfo`).
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Creation: It calls `socket()` to create a communication endpoint and receives a file
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] descriptor.
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Connection: It calls `connect()` to establish a TCP handshake with the destination IP
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] and port.
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Transfer: It uses `send()` (or `write()`) to transmit the HTTP request and `recv()` (or
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] `read()`) to ingest the response.
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  Termination: It calls `close()` to tear down the connection.
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] • Library location*
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] The `socket` function is part of the standard C library, typically `glibc` (GNU C Library)
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] on most Linux distributions. This library provides the API wrappers that invoke the actual
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] underlying kernel system calls.
2025-12-20 07:29:04 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:19 [nitro@ryzen5] same with connect?
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] • How curl uses sockets on Linux*
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] `curl` operates in user space and interacts with the Linux kernel's networking stack using system
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] calls. The general process involves:
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  socket(): Creates a socket file descriptor (an endpoint for communication).
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  connect(): Initiates a connection to the target IP address and port (e.g., 8081).
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  send()/write(): Sends the HTTP request data through the socket.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  recv()/read(): Receives the response data from the server.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  close(): Terminates the connection.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] • Library location for `socket`*
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] The `socket` function is a system call wrapper provided by the Standard C Library (commonly
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] `libc` or `glibc` on Linux). It is defined in `<sys/socket.h>`.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] • Library location for `connect`*
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] Similarly, `connect` is also a system call wrapper provided by `libc` (or `glibc`).
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] It is also defined in `<sys/socket.h>`.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] While these functions reside in `libc`, they ultimately invoke the underlying Linux kernel system
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] calls to perform the actual networking operations.
2025-12-20 07:29:33 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:31:04 [nitro@ryzen5] what was wrong with wget in 1998?
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] In 1998, Wget (specifically the 1.5.x series) had several security and functional limitations that
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] were addressed in later versions:
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Security Vulnerabilities: It contained flaws regarding symbolic link handling and buffer
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] overflows (such as those addressed in CVE-2000-0517). These allowed malicious servers to
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] potentially overwrite local files on the client machine.
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Protocol Limitations: It primarily relied on HTTP/1.0 and had only experimental, limited
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] support for SSL (HTTPS).
2025-12-20 07:31:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:32:00 [nitro@ryzen5] was there anything curl could do wget couldn't
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] In 1998, there was nothing technically "wrong" with wget; it was a robust tool designed
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] specifically for mirroring websites and downloading files recursively to a local disk. However, it
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] had a different design philosophy than curl, which led to functional differences.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] Here is what curl could do that wget generally could not (or did not focus on) at
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] that time:
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Standard Output (stdout): Curl was designed to pipe data to standard output by default.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] This allowed users to process data immediately in a terminal pipeline (e.g., `curl url | grep
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] pattern`). Wget defaulted to saving files to disk.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Upload Capabilities: Curl offered robust support for uploading data (via HTTP POST/PUT
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] and FTP uploads), whereas Wget was primarily a download-only tool.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Library Integration: Curl was developed alongside libcurl, a library intended
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] to be embedded in other programs to handle network transfers. Wget was designed strictly as a
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] standalone command-line utility.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  Protocol Support: Curl aimed to support a wider variety of transfer protocols (such as
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] GOPHER, DICT, TELNET, and LDAP) beyond the HTTP and FTP focus of Wget.
2025-12-20 07:32:14 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:35:11 [nitro@ryzen5] wget was not available on some platforms?
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] In 1998, `wget` was a robust tool for recursive website mirroring, but it differed significantly
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] from `curl` in design and capability.
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a summary of the differences and limitations at that time:
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] • Data Uploads: `wget` was strictly a downloader. It could not perform HTTP POST or PUT
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] requests, nor could it handle FTP uploads. `curl` was designed to transfer data in both directions.
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] • Piping: `curl` defaults to printing output to `stdout`, allowing it to function as a pipe
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] in command-line chains. `wget` defaults to saving files to the disk.
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] • Library Support: `curl` was powered by a reusable library (`libcurl`), allowing its
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] functionality to be embedded in other applications. `wget` was a standalone command-line
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] executable.
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] • Portability: `wget` was primarily developed for Unix environments. `curl` prioritized
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] high portability and compiled on a wider range of non-Unix platforms (such as AmigaOS and OS/2)
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] earlier than `wget`.
2025-12-20 07:35:30 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:22:59 [nitro@ryzen5] with rp2040 calling the gpio_init stops the adcs from working correctly?
2025-12-20 08:23:00 [nitro@ryzen5] 	adc_gpio_init(ADC0_PIN);	adc_gpio_init(ADC1_PIN);
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, calling `gpio_init()` on an ADC pin will stop the ADC from reading correctly.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] `gpio_init()` configures the pin for digital SIO (Software I/O), which often enables digital input
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] buffers and default pull-ups/downs that distort analog signals.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] To use the ADC, you must use the specific initialization sequence:
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Call `adc_init()` once to enable the ADC hardware block.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Call `adcgpioinit(pin)` (as shown in your snippet) to set the pin to High Impedance
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] mode and disable digital buffers.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Select the input with `adcselectinput(input_index)`.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] Ensure you do not call `gpio_init()` on those pins after setting them up for ADC.
2025-12-20 08:23:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:32:52 [nitro@ryzen5] who declared wget deprecated first?
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] • RP2040 ADC Initialization*
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, calling `gpio_init()` on the RP2040 ADC pins (GP26–GP29) will stop the ADC from reading
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] correctly.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  The Cause: `gpio_init()` configures the pin for digital I/O (Function 5, SIO).
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] Crucially, this enables the digital input buffer (Schmitt trigger) and often sets a default
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] drive strength. This digital circuitry is connected in parallel with the ADC input, lowering the
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] input impedance and causing current leakage that distorts the analog voltage signal.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  The Solution: You must use `adcgpioinit()`. This function explicitly disables
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] the digital input buffer and disconnects the digital pad circuitry, placing the pin in a
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] High-Impedance (Hi-Z) mode required for accurate analog measurements.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] • Regarding Wget*
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] GNU Wget has not been declared deprecated. It is actively maintained by the Free Software
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] Foundation and remains a standard utility in most Linux distributions.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] There are, however, common misconceptions regarding its status:
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] • macOS: Apple has generally excluded `wget` from the base OS in favor of `curl`, but this
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] is a distribution choice, not a deprecation of the tool itself.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] • Windows PowerShell: Microsoft created an alias where `wget` executed `Invoke-WebRequest`.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] This was often criticized for confusing scripts, but it was an aliasing issue, not a deprecation of
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] the GNU binary.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] • Wget2: A successor, `wget2` (based on libwget), exists and offers multithreading, but the
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] original `wget` remains the stable standard.
2025-12-20 08:33:26 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:36:02 [nitro@ryzen5] adc.h says this:
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, calling `gpio_init` on an ADC pin will stop the ADC from working correctly.
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] On the RP2040, `gpio_init` configures the pin for digital use (SIO function), which enables digital
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] input buffers and often sets default pulls. This interferes with the analog signal.
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] You should exclusively use `adcgpioinit` for analog pins. This function ensures the pin is
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] set to a high-impedance state (floating) with digital buffers disabled, which is required for
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] accurate analog measurements.
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] If you call `gpioinit` after `adcgpio_init`, you are re-enabling the digital
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] circuitry, which overrides the analog configuration.
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] Regarding `wget`: It is generally not deprecated and remains a standard GNU tool, though `curl` is
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] often preferred in modern development environments. This query appears to be a separate context
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] pasted by accident.
2025-12-20 08:36:23 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:14 [nitro@ryzen5] calling adc_gpio_init on my pico pins stops the adc functioning? i don't understand the code in their header
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] The `adcgpioinit` function disconnects the digital input/output hardware attached to that
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] pin. This is necessary to prevent digital noise from interfering with the analog measurement and to
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] prevent current leakage.
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] If the ADC appears to stop functioning, it is usually because of a mismatch between the GPIO
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] number and the ADC Channel number.
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO 26 maps to ADC Channel 0
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO 27 maps to ADC Channel 1
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO 28 maps to ADC Channel 2
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] • GPIO 29 maps to ADC Channel 3
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] A common mistake is calling `adcselectinput(26)` instead of `adcselectinput(0)`.
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a reusable C function that handles the initialization and channel selection correctly.
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] #include "hardware/adc.h"
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] void enable_adc_on_gpio(uint gpio_pin) {
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Ensure the pin is valid for ADC (GPIO 26-29)
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	if (gpio_pin < 26 || gpio_pin > 29) {
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 		return;
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	}
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 1. Initialize the ADC hardware
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	adc_init();
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 2. Prepare the GPIO for analog use (disables digital input/output)
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	adc_gpio_init(gpio_pin);
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 3. Select the correct input channel based on GPIO number
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	// GPIO 26 is Channel 0, so we subtract 26
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	adc_select_input(gpio_pin - 26);
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] uint16_t read_adc_value(void) {
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 	return adc_read();
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-20 08:37:34 [gemini-3-pro] 
