2025-12-20 08:41:49 [nitro@ryzen5] to read the atari paddles correctly
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] The behavior you are seeing is intentional within the RP2040 SDK, though it conflicts with your
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] specific hardware setup for Atari paddles.
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] • Why this happens:*
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] The `adcgpioinit` function explicitly disables both internal pull-up and pull-down
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] resistors. It does this to create a "Hi-Z" (high impedance) input, ensuring that the internal
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] resistors do not alter the voltage coming from standard active sensors.
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] • Why your fix works:*
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] Atari paddles are passive variable resistors (potentiometers). To measure them with an ADC, you
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] need a voltage divider circuit. By calling `gpiosetpulls`, you are using the Pico's
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] internal resistor (approx. 50kΩ) as the ground leg of that voltage divider.
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] Since `adcgpioinit` turns that resistor off, your code to turn it back on is necessary
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] unless you add physical external resistors.
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a reusable function to handle this configuration safely:
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] void adc_paddle_init(uint pin) {
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Initialize the ADC hardware for this pin (disables pulls by default)
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 	adc_gpio_init(pin);
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Re-enable the internal pull-down to act as the voltage divider
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_set_pulls(pin, false, true);
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:42:09 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:22 [nitro@ryzen5] for another use case, measuring wiper pin of 10K Piher horizontal trimpot
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] The behavior you are seeing is expected due to how `adcgpioinit` configures the hardware
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] versus how Atari paddles are constructed.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] • Why `adcgpioinit` "breaks" your setup*
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] The SDK implementation of `adcgpioinit` performs the following actions:
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Sets the pin to a high-impedance input (High-Z).
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Disables both internal pull-up and pull-down resistors.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] It disables the resistors because, for a standard 3-pin potentiometer (voltage divider), internal
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] pulls would introduce error by altering the resistance ratio.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] • Why your fix works*
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] Atari paddles are typically variable resistors (2-pin rheostats), not voltage dividers.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] They do not have a built-in connection to Ground.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Without a pull-down resistor, the pin is pulled to VCC through the paddle but has no path to
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] Ground. The ADC sees a constant high voltage.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  By calling `gpiosetpulls(..., false, true)`, you re-enable the internal pull-down
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] resistor. This creates a voltage divider (Paddle Resistance vs. Internal Pull-Down), allowing the
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] voltage to vary as you turn the knob.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] • Recommendation*
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] For the 10K Piher trimpot, if you wire it as a standard 3-pin voltage divider (VCC, Wiper, GND),
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] you should use the standard `adcgpioinit` without re-enabling pulls.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] If you are using the Atari paddle, you can wrap your initialization to ensure the specific
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] configuration applies only to those pins.
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] void init_paddle_adc(uint gpio_pin) {
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Initialize the ADC pin (disables pulls by default)
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 	adc_gpio_init(gpio_pin);
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 	// Re-enable internal pull-down to create a voltage divider for the variable resistor
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_set_pulls(gpio_pin, false, true);
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:44:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-20 08:57:55 [nitro@ryzen5] kimi can you summarise conversation about gpio and ignore the wget bit
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] - `adcgpioinit()` puts the pin in true high-impedance mode (both internal pulls OFF).
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] - Atari paddles are 2-terminal variable resistors; without a pull-down the ADC line just sits at
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] VDD.
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] - Re-enabling the internal pull-down (`gpiosetpulls(pin, false, true)`) gives the needed
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] second resistor so the wiper voltage can swing.
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] - Ordinary 3-pin pots wired as voltage dividers don’t need the extra pull-down call; leave the pin
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] in the default hi-Z state that `adcgpioinit()` sets.
2025-12-20 08:58:11 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:19 [nitro@ryzen5] the trimpot is very noisy
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Noise on a trimpot wiper is almost always a mechanical or electrical problem, not the ADC
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] itself.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Typical fixes, in order of impact:
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Hardware
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - 100 nF ceramic from wiper to GND (right at the pin).
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Use the 3.3 V rail, not VBUS / 5 V.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Keep wiper wire < 2 cm and away from digital traces.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - If the shaft is accessible, add a 1 kΩ–4.7 kΩ fixed resistor between VCC and one end of the
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] pot so the wiper never hits the supply rail (reduces scratch).
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Firmware (cheap digital filter)
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    Exponential IIR, one multiply, no buffer:
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] static uint16_t filter(uint16_t raw, uint16_t *state, uint8_t shift)
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     /* shift = 3 → τ ≈ 8 samples, 4 → 16 samples, etc. */
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     *state += ((raw - *state) >> shift);
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]     return *state;
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Call every time you read:
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] uint16_t quiet = filter(adc_read(), &pot_state, 3);
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. ADC-side
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Run the ADC at ≤ 500 kSa/s (default is fine).
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Take 4–8 samples in rapid succession and average; the filter above already does the same job.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905]    - Enable the RP2040 ADC reference buffer (`adcsettempsensorenabled(false)`) if
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] you’re not using the temp sensor—adds ~ 1 LSB of noise rejection.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Do the capacitor first; the single-pole IIR afterwards is usually enough to make the reading
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] rock-steady.
2025-12-20 09:11:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:31:37 [nitro@ryzen5] testing atari paddles with pico 2040 adc, the paddles are not linear response?
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] The 2040’s ADC is 12-bit but only ~8-9 bits are really linear; the rest is noise and DNL.
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Paddles add another non-linearity: the 100 kΩ pot + 150 kΩ pull-up inside the CX-40 forms a voltage
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] divider that is already S-shaped (log-ish) near the ends.
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] So the raw count you see is a compound of:
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. ADC INL/DNL
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Pot law (ratiometric, not linear)
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. 3.3 V vs original 5 V range (you’re using only ~2/3 of the ADC range)
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Cheap fix in firmware: calibrate each paddle once, then map the min/max counts to 0-255 (or
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 0-65535) with a linear interpolation.
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want the visual cursor to feel linear to the hand, apply an inverse-square or cubic
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] correction table (five or six points are enough).
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] Code snippet (C, SDK):
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] static uint16_t calib_lo[4], calib_hi[4];
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] void paddle_calibrate(uint ch)
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	calib_lo[ch] = adc_read();   // ask user to turn full CCW
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	sleep_ms(100);
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	calib_hi[ch] = adc_read();   // ask user to turn full CW
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] uint8_t paddle_read(uint ch)
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	uint16_t v = adc_read();
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (v < calib_lo[ch]) v = calib_lo[ch];
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (v > calib_hi[ch]) v = calib_hi[ch];
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	uint32_t t = (v - calib_lo[ch]) * 255U;
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return t / (calib_hi[ch] - calib_lo[ch]);
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] If you still want better than 8-bit precision, oversample 4× and decimate (gives ~1 extra bit), or
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] use an external 16-bit delta-sigma ADC.
2025-12-20 10:32:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:21 [nitro@ryzen5] explain pot law
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] “Pot law” is short for taper law – the deliberate non-linear resistance profile machined
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] into the carbon track.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Linear taper (B)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]    Wiper position → resistance changes in equal steps.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]    Voltage out vs shaft angle is a straight line (ideal for servo feedback, lab PSU).
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Log/audio taper (A)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]    Resistance changes slowly at one end, quickly at the other.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]    Matches human loudness perception; 50 % rotation ≈ 10 % resistance, so volume feels “even”.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Reverse-log (C)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]    Mirror of audio taper; rare, used for balance controls.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Atari CX-40 paddles use cheap “pseudo-log” tracks: two different linear slopes joined in
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] the middle.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Electrically you have:
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]         3.3 V
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]           │
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]         150 kΩ (inside console)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]           │
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]           ├─── ADC pin
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]           │
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]         100 kΩ pot
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]           │
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]          GND
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Transfer function:
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905]         Vout = 3.3 V × Rpot / (150 k + R_pot)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Derivative dV/dR is largest when Rpot ≈ 0 (CCW) and smallest when Rpot ≈ 100 k (CW).
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Result: voltage changes fast near CCW, slow near CW – an S-shaped curve even if the track itself
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] were perfectly linear.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] So “pot law” here means:
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] - Mechanical taper (pseudo-log carbon)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] - Electrical loading (fixed pull-up)
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] Both make the ADC code non-linear with shaft angle.
2025-12-20 10:33:47 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:28:43 [nitro@ryzen5] with pico2040 can i set up counter for gpio changes
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes.
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] The RP2040 gives each GPIO pin a dedicated set of hardware “sticky” edge-detect bits.
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] You can therefore build a simple counter in software that is updated entirely by the PIO (or by the
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] main core) without burning cycles polling the pin.
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a minimal, self-contained example that counts every edge (both rising and falling)
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] on a single GPIO.
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] It uses one PIO state-machine; the CPU just reads the 32-bit value whenever it wants.
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] C code (SDK 2.x, single file, builds with `cmake`)
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] #include "pico/stdlib.h"
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] #include "hardware/pio.h"
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] #include "count_edges.pio.h"   // generated by pioasm
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] #define PIN  14                // change to any valid GPIO
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] PIO   pio = pio0;
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] uint  sm;                      // state-machine index
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] int main(void)
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] {
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	stdio_init_all();
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	// load the 1-instruction PIO program
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &count_edges_program);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	sm = pio_claim_unused_sm(pio, true);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	count_edges_program_init(pio, sm, offset, PIN);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	while (true)
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 	{
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 		// 32-bit counter lives in the TX FIFO
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 		if (pio_sm_get_rx_fifo_level(pio, sm))
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 		{
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 			uint32_t edges = pio_sm_get(pio, sm);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 			printf("edges = %lu\n", edges);
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 		}
2025-12-20 12:29:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 		sleep_ms(500);
