2025-12-20 12:41:43 [nitro@ryzen5] can we mod the pio code to test GP2
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a drop-in replacement that counts both edges on GP2.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Only the PIO source changes; the C host code stays identical (just make sure you call
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] `countedgesprogram_init(pio, sm, offset, 2);` so the PIO state machine observes GP2).
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Save as `countedgesgp2.pio`, assemble with
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] `pioasm countedgesgp2.pio count_edges.pio.h` (or your local path) and rebuild the project.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] countedgesgp2.pio
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ; Count every edge (rise + fall) on GP2
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ; Side-set is free for future use, but not required here
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] .program count_edges
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] .side_set 1 opt
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] public start:
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     set x, 0                 ; clear local counter
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] loop:
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     wait 0 pin 2             ; wait for low
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     wait 1 pin 2             ; low→high edge
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     jmp count_rise
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] count_rise:
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     set x, x                 ; dummy (1 cycle) so fall path matches length
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     in x, 1                  ; push a 1 into ISR
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     wait 1 pin 2             ; stay while high
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     wait 0 pin 2             ; high→low edge
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     in x, 1                  ; push another 1 into ISR
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     jmp push_check
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] push_check:
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     ; whenever ISR has 32 bits, send to RX FIFO
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     push block               ; automatically clears ISR
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     set x, 0                 ; reset local tally
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905]     jmp loop
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] What changed / why it works
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. `pin 2` everywhere → hardware now watches GP2.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Two `wait` blocks in series give a full high-time window so both rising and falling edges are
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] captured.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Each edge shifts a single ‘1’ into the input shift register (ISR).
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. When 32 bits accumulate, `push block` delivers the 32-bit count to the CPU via the RX FIFO and
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] clears ISR for the next batch.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] No external components needed; if the signal on GP2 bounces, add an RC filter or increase the
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] sample time in software.
2025-12-20 12:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:02 [nitro@ryzen5] what does side_set do?
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] `side_set` lets the PIO emit a second, synchronous signal on every instruction
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] without spending an extra cycle.
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] How it works
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. You declare how many pins you want to drive in parallel with the main instruction:
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905]    `.side_set 1`  ← 1 extra pin
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905]    `.side_set 2`  ← 2 extra pins, etc.
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. In the instruction you append the pin state(s) after the side-set delimiter `side`:
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905]    jmp loop side 1        ; assert side-pin HIGH while the jump executes
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905]    nop side 0             ; keep it LOW during the nop
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. The side pin(s) change in the same clock edge that the instruction retires, so you get
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] precise, jitter-free timing (useful for clocks, strobes, chip-selects, differential outputs, etc.).
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. If you mark it `opt` (`.side_set 1 opt`) you may omit the `side` qualifier on any instruction;
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] when omitted the side pins simply hold their previous value.
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] In the edge-counter program we declared `.side_set 1 opt` but never used the qualifier, so the side
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] pin remains constant and costs nothing—leaving it free for future extensions (e.g. blink an LED or
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] generate a sampling clock).
2025-12-20 12:44:28 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-20 12:45:07 [nitro@ryzen5] so i can remove to keep things simple
2025-12-20 12:45:12 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—if you’re not driving anything with it, just delete the declaration and every `side` keyword.
2025-12-20 12:45:12 [kimi-k2-0905] The counter works identically and the PIO file becomes two lines shorter.
