2025-12-20 14:33:31 [scudmarks@discord] on the GP2 pin i would like to send time stamp for each rising and falling edge
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] Sure, detecting rising and falling edges on GP2 and timestamping them is a great PIO use case for
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] precise timing.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] Quick Overview
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] - Use a PIO state machine to monitor GP2 for level changes.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] - Capture the timestamp using the PIO's timer (e.g., via `get()` or integrated timer instructions).
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] - For each edge, you can push the timestamp to a FIFO or trigger an interrupt to the main core.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] Steps to Implement
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 1. Configure the Pin: Set GP2 as an input in your main code (e.g., via `gpioinit()` and
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] `gpioset_dir()`).
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 2. PIO Program: Write a PIO program that waits for rising/falling edges and captures the
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] current timestamp.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1]    - Use `wait` for pin changes, then `mov` to capture the timer value.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1]    - Push to FIFO for reading by the CPU.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 3. Main Code: Initialize the PIO, load the program, set up the state machine, and read
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] timestamps from the FIFO in a loop or via interrupts.
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] If you'd like example code or a specific PIO assembly snippet, just request it—let me know the
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] language (C/C++?) and any other details!
2025-12-20 14:33:38 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:34:16 [scudmarks@discord] is there a generic clock count i can read?
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] Yes, the RP2040's PIO can access a 64-bit system clock counter via the `get()` instruction in PIO
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] assembly. This counter increments at the system clock rate (typically 125 MHz on Pico) and provides
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] a timestamp.
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] In your PIO program, use something like:
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] - `get(y, systime)` to read the lower 32 bits into register y.
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] - Or `get(y, systime_upper)` for the upper 32 bits if needed.
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] This gives you the exact clock count at the time of the edge. For full precision, combine both
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] parts.
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] If you need code examples or integration tips, let me know!
2025-12-20 14:34:21 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:35:28 [scudmarks@discord] nice, can we sample that and send lowest 10 bits to fifo?
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] Yes, absolutely. In your PIO program, after detecting an edge:
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 1. Sample the lower 32 bits of the system timer into a register (e.g., `get(y, systime)`).
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 2. Mask to get the lowest 10 bits (e.g., `and(y, y, 0x3FF)` which is 1023 in decimal).
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 3. Push to the FIFO (e.g., `push(block)` to send y).
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] This gives you a 10-bit timestamp (rolling over every 1024 clock cycles) pushed for each edge.
2025-12-20 14:35:34 [grok-code-1] 
