2025-12-20 14:37:34 [scudmarks@discord] please explain push block
2025-12-20 14:37:39 [grok-code-1] In RP2040 PIO assembly, `push block` moves data from the Output Shift Register (OSR) to the TX
2025-12-20 14:37:39 [grok-code-1] FIFO. The `block` modifier makes the state machine pause if the FIFO is full, waiting for space
2025-12-20 14:37:39 [grok-code-1] before continuing. This ensures no data is lost but can stall the PIO if the CPU doesn't read
2025-12-20 14:37:39 [grok-code-1] quickly. Without `block`, it would discard data if full.
2025-12-20 14:37:39 [grok-code-1] 
2025-12-20 14:38:05 [scudmarks@discord] how big is the register i am pushing?
2025-12-20 14:38:10 [grok-code-1] The `push block` instruction sends the full 32-bit contents of the Output Shift Register (OSR) to
2025-12-20 14:38:10 [grok-code-1] the TX FIFO. In your code, you're loading a 10-bit masked value into register y, then (implicitly)
2025-12-20 14:38:10 [grok-code-1] moving it to OSR before pushing—so the FIFO entry is 32 bits wide, with your data in the lowest 10
2025-12-20 14:38:10 [grok-code-1] bits. The rest are zeros or undefined.
2025-12-20 14:38:10 [grok-code-1] 
