2025-12-21 09:16:31 [nitro@ryzen5] this code does not loop?
2025-12-21 09:16:32 [nitro@ryzen5] .program luma2wrap:  mov isr null  wait 0 pin 0  mov x isr  push  mov isr null  wait 1 pin 0  mov x isr  push
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] You're right - that PIO program doesn't loop. PIO programs only loop if:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 1. You explicitly use a `loop` label with a block structure
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 2. The FIFO is kept full by the CPU (for pull/push heavy programs)
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] Your program just runs once through and halts. Here's the fix with an explicit loop:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] .program luma2wrap:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]     loop:                           ; Loop label
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         wait    0 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         push
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         wait    1 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         push                    ; jumps back to 'loop' label
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] • Key points:*
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] - `loop:` label marks the loop start
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] - After `push`, PIO automatically jumps back to the `loop:` label
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] - No explicit `jump loop` instruction needed
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] Or if you want it more explicit:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] .program luma2wrap:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]     loop:
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         ; ... your code ...
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         push
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1]         jump    loop
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] Load it with DMA or CPU keeping TX FIFO full for continuous operation.
2025-12-21 09:16:37 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:26 [nitro@ryzen5] explain the rp wrap mechanism
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] RP2040 PIO Wrap Mechanism
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] The wrap mechanism is PIO's elegant way to create infinite loops without explicit `jump`
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] instructions. It's configured in hardware when you load the program.
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] How it works:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] .program myloop:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]     entry_point:    ; <- WRAP_TARGET (default: instruction 0)
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         ; your loop code here
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         ; ...
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]     wrap_point:     ; <- WRAP (must be AFTER entry_point)
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         nop
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] • Key registers (set when loading program):*
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] - `WRAP_TARGET` - where execution starts (usually instruction 0)
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] - `WRAP` - instruction after your loop ends
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] - Execution jumps from `WRAP` → `WRAP_TARGET` automatically
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] Visual example:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] Instr#  Label       Code
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]   0     entry:     mov x, isr
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]   1                   wait 0 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]   2                   push       ← execution continues to instr#3
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]   3     wrap:      nop         ← WRAP points here
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]                       ^ jumps back to instr#0
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] Your fixed code with explicit wrap:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] .program luma2wrap:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]     entry:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         wait    0 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         push           ; exec → next instr
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]     wrap:
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         wait    1 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1]         push           ; WRAP→ jumps to 'entry'
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] Loading with wrap (C SDK):
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] pio_sm_config c = pio_get_default_sm_config();
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] sm_config_set_wrap(&c, /*target*/ 0, /*wrap*/ 4);  // instr 0→4
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] pio_sm_init(pio0, 0, wrap_offset, &c);
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] • Default*: `wrap_target=0`, `wrap=32` (runs once). Always set wrap explicitly for loops!
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] This is why your original code didn't loop - no wrap configured.
2025-12-21 09:17:33 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:20 [nitro@ryzen5] i want simplest default wrapping option pls
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] • Simplest default wrapping - put your loop code in instructions 0-31*, nothing
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] else needed:
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] .program luma2wrap:
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     ; Instructions 0-31 = your loop (runs forever)
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     wait    0 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     push
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     mov     isr, null
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     wait    1 pin 0
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     mov     x, isr
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     push
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     ; ... pad with nops to 32 instructions total ...
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1]     nop  [29]  ; fills remaining 29 slots
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] • C SDK load (defaults handle wrapping):*
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] pio_sm_config c = pio_get_default_sm_config();
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] sm_config_set_wrap(&c, 0, 32);  // DEFAULT: 0→32 (automatic!)
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] pio_sm_init(pio0, 0, wrap_offset, &c);
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
2025-12-21 09:20:25 [grok-4-1] 
