2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, I am familiar with the RP2040 PIO assembly language.
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] JMP on Pin Low
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] There is no direct instruction to "jump if pin is low." The instruction `jmp pin, label` jumps only
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] if the selected JMP pin is High (1).
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] To handle a Low (0) state, you typically structure the code to fall through the
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] instruction:
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp pin, is_high    ; If pin is 1, jump to is_high
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp is_low          ; If pin is 0, fall through to here (and jump to handler)
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] JMP X-- Logic
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] The instruction `jmp x--, label` is a post-decrement scratch register check, commonly used for
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] loops or timeouts.
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] The logic proceeds as follows:
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Check: The state machine checks if the value in register X is non-zero.
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Decrement: It decrements the value in X by 1 (regardless of the check result).
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Action:
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro]     *   If X was non-zero (before decrementing), it jumps to the target label.
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro]     *   If X was zero, it does nothing and falls through to the next instruction.
2025-12-21 18:18:58 [gemini-3-pro] 
