2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] There is no specific `jmp not_pin` instruction. `jmp pin, label` only jumps if the mapped pin is
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] High (1).
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] To execute code when a pin is Low (0), you typically structure the logic to fall through
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] the instruction:
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp pin, is_high
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro]     ; Code here executes if pin is LOW
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro]     jmp continue
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] is_high:
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro]     ; Code here executes if pin is HIGH
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] continue:
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] • 2. `jmp x--` Logic*
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] This is a post-decrement conditional jump.
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] • If X is not 0: It decrements X by 1, then jumps to the target label.
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] • If X is 0: It does not decrement and does not jump (it falls through to
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] the next line).
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] • 3. Is there a `clr x`?*
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] No, there is no `clr` opcode. To clear X, use:
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] set x, 0
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] Or, if you need to clear it using the OSR or other sources:
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] mov x, null
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-21 18:20:43 [gemini-3-pro] 
