2025-12-23 22:33:06 [gemini-3-pro] The value of `y` will be equal to the current contents of the Input Shift Register (ISR).
2025-12-23 22:33:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:33:06 [gemini-3-pro] The final instruction `mov y, isr` unconditionally overwrites the value `6` set by the previous
2025-12-23 22:33:06 [gemini-3-pro] instruction. If the ISR has not been populated with data, the value will be 0.
2025-12-23 22:33:06 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:33:49 [nitro@ryzen5] can i clr isr? shift y into lower 6 bits and then move the result back into y?
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] I am proficient in RP2040 PIO assembly and can assist with state machine configuration and
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] instruction sets.
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] • Value of Y*
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] After the instruction `mov y, isr`, the register `y` will hold the current value of the ISR
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] (Input Shift Register). The previous values set by `mov y, !null` and `set y, 6` are overwritten.
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] • Clearing ISR*
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, you can clear the ISR using the following instruction:
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] mov isr, null
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] • Shifting/Masking Y*
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, you can isolate the lower 6 bits of `y` by using the ISR as a temporary buffer. Assuming your
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] state machine is configured to shift right (shifting into LSBs), the sequence is:
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] mov isr, null   ; Clear the ISR
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] in y, 6         ; Shift 6 bits from Y into the ISR
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] mov y, isr      ; Copy the ISR value back to Y
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:34:11 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:36:24 [nitro@ryzen5] so y should be 63?
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] In the snippet provided, `y` will simply hold the current value of the `isr`. The instruction `mov
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] y, isr` overwrites the results of the previous `mov` and `set` commands.
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] To implement the logic you described (clearing the ISR and shifting 6 bits of `!null` into it) to
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] result in 63, you should use the `in` instruction.
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the corrected sequence:
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] mov		y, !null	; Set y to -1 (all 1s)
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] mov		isr, null	; Clear isr
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] in		y, 6		; Shift 6 bits of y into isr (isr becomes 0x3F)
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] mov		y, isr		; Copy 63 back to y
2025-12-23 22:36:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
