2025-12-29 15:30:12 [nitro@ryzen5] what is ws in ws2812
2025-12-29 15:30:13 [kimi-k2-0711] In WS2812 the “WS” stands for WorldSemi – the name of the company that originally designed the
2025-12-29 15:30:13 [kimi-k2-0711] LED/driver IC.
2025-12-29 15:30:13 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-29 15:30:27 [nitro@ryzen5] who is worldsemi?
2025-12-29 15:30:30 [kimi-k2-0711] WorldSemi Co. Ltd. (a.k.a. WorldSemi Semiconductor) is a small fabless IC-design house founded in
2025-12-29 15:30:30 [kimi-k2-0711] Shenzhen, China around 2011.
2025-12-29 15:30:30 [kimi-k2-0711] They created the first integrated single-wire 5050 RGB LED + driver IC (WS2811/WS2812 family) that
2025-12-29 15:30:30 [kimi-k2-0711] became the de-facto standard for addressable LED strips and arrays.
2025-12-29 15:30:30 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-29 15:31:18 [nitro@ryzen5] is 2812 most popular?
2025-12-29 15:31:20 [kimi-k2-0711] Yes.
2025-12-29 15:31:20 [kimi-k2-0711] WS2812 (and its 5-V clone “NeoPixel”) dominate the hobby, maker, and small-production lighting
2025-12-29 15:31:20 [kimi-k2-0711] market because they’re the cheapest, easiest to drive with a single-wire 800 kHz signal, and have
2025-12-29 15:31:20 [kimi-k2-0711] the widest ecosystem (Arduino, Raspberry Pi, ESP-IDF, FastLED, WLED, etc.).
2025-12-29 15:31:20 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:28:03 [scudmarks@discord] hi sloppy you are in general training data a discord channel
2025-12-30 11:28:03 [kimi-k2-0711] I’m here—what do you need?
2025-12-30 11:28:03 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:28:32 [scudmarks@discord] can discord provide some stordage for bots?
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] Yes, Discord bots can read/store small amounts of persistent data through Guild features:
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] • Channel topics (1024 chars)
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] • Role names/colours/permissions
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] • Voice region, verification level etc.
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] Nothing like a key/value bucket—every change is public and rate-limited.
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] If you need real storage, host your own (file, Redis, Postgres) or use a free tier (Supabase,
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] Cloudflare KV, PlanetScale, etc.).
2025-12-30 11:28:34 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:29:29 [scudmarks@discord] how does having more than 1 bot work in a channel?
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Discord treats every bot as a normal user account (a “bot user”).
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] You can add as many bots as you like to the same server and channel; they all share the same
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] message stream and permission model.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Typical setup
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. Create each bot in the Developer Portal → “New Application” → “Bot” tab.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. Invite each bot with its own OAuth2 URL (scope=bot, plus the permissions it needs).
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 3. Every bot logs in with its own token and receives the same events (messages, reactions, etc.)
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] that the permission tree lets it see.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 4. In code you can ignore other bots by checking msg.author.bot (true for any bot message,
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] including your own).
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 5. There is no cross-bot coordination; they race like any other user.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] Limitations
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] • Global rate limits (50 requests / second per token) and per-route limits apply per bot, not per
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] guild.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] • If two bots share the same prefix (e.g., “!”) they’ll both respond unless you explicitly
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] de-duplicate in code.
2025-12-30 11:29:33 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:31:43 [scudmarks@discord] is there discord etiquette for inviting other people to a channel?
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] Yes, the usual etiquette:
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] • Ask first – a quick DM or @ mention to the server owner or the person who runs the channel.
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] • Explain why – “I made a small music bot for our study group; may I add it?”
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] • Be clear on permissions – list the scopes it needs (Send Messages, Embed Links, etc.) so they
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] don’t have to guess.
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] • Drop the invite link, don’t paste the token – never share the bot token in chat.
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] • Test in a private channel first – keeps spam away from public channels until it’s stable.
2025-12-30 11:31:46 [kimi-k2-0711] 
